(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m3-n101"]={"id":"n101","label":"Notes 10.1 · bold main idea first","revision":"ling406-modern-m3-n101-2","scripts":{"m3-reading1-p001":[],"m3-reading1-p002":[{"text":"**The first thing to remember about phonemes is that phonemes are not letters.** A **phoneme** (a speech-sound category) belongs to spoken language. A letter belongs to a writing system.","children":["Writing represents language, but its symbols do not have to match phonemes one to one.","One letter can take part in different pronunciations.","One phoneme can be written with different spellings.","Identifying a letter is a different task from identifying a phoneme.","The question concerns how sound relates to writing, not how many letters a word has."]}],"m3-reading1-p003":[{"text":"**Scripts differ in how reliably spelling gives pronunciation, and English is a deep script.**","children":["In a **mispronouncing contest**, people willfully mispronounced words like COLONEL and PARADIGM.","Usual letter patterns give the wrong pronunciation for both words.","So phonemes are not letters, and letters are not phonemes either."]},{"text":"This difference between scripts is **orthographic depth** (how consistently spelling matches pronunciation).","children":["**English** is relatively **deep** because of its irregularities.","**Finnish** and **Korean** are very **shallow** scripts.","**Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are relatively regular.","A deep script is less predictable, not random.","The difference is in the writing system, not in the readers' ability."]}],"m3-reading1-p004":[{"text":"**In Finnish, each letter matches one phoneme, so Finnish children learn to decode very quickly.** Finnish is very **transparent**, or **shallow** in the terms of the **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis**.","children":["Each letter corresponds more or less unambiguously to one phoneme.","Following a beginning Finnish lesson with its text makes the pattern clear within about a minute.","A doubled consonant stands for a **geminated** consonant (a consonant held longer).","A doubled vowel stands for a **long vowel**.","**Decoding** (producing a pronunciation from a written form) is fast to learn.","Consistent matches let children read written forms they have never seen."]}],"m3-reading1-p005":[{"text":"**Children learn to decode more slowly in English than in most other Roman-alphabet languages.** English is an **outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet.","children":["**Aro and Wimmer** had children read **pseudowords** (made-up words that follow the spelling and sound patterns of their language).","A pseudoword is not a stored word, so reading it tests decoding.","In **Grade 1**, French, German, Dutch, Spanish, Swedish and Finnish learners read about 85 to 93 percent accurately.","In Grade 1, **English** learners read only about **half** accurately.","By Grade 4, English learners reach about 88 percent.","Less predictable spelling-sound matches explain the slower start.","English children can still decode. They just learn it more slowly."]}],"m3-reading1-p006":[{"text":"**In Korean Hangul, similar phonemes have similar symbols, so the angular ㄱ shape marks velar consonants.** Korean is even more regular than Finnish.","children":["Letters sit in **syllable blocks** with an **initial**, a **medial** and sometimes a **final** position.","**Velar** consonants (made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate) share the angular ㄱ shape.","ㄱ is g.","Doubling it gives ㄲ, which is kk.","Adding a stroke gives ㅋ, which is k.","So the letter shapes show sound structure, not only consistent pronunciation."]}],"m3-reading1-p007":[{"text":"**Readers can pronounce pseudowords like GLORP, SPLING, JONK, FEESH and QUISP, which puts decoding into action.**","children":["These items are not English words.","Each follows enough English spelling patterns for a **plausible pronunciation**.","No stored pronunciation exists for them, so success cannot come from memory.","Readers use knowledge that **generalizes** across words.","The items test **decoding**, not vocabulary or meaning."]}],"m3-reading1-p008":[{"text":"**Nonwords are read nearly as quickly and accurately as real words, so decoding is natural, automatic and implicit.**","children":["**Lupker, Brown and Colombo (1997)** measured **naming time** (how quickly a pronunciation becomes available when reading aloud).","They compared pseudowords, here called **nonwords**, with real words.","Nonword naming was only **5 to 12 percent slower** than for low-frequency words.","The baseline is low-frequency words, not all real words."]},{"text":"A later question is how to characterize decoding.","children":["Computational models of word reading ask whether it fits **categorical rules** or **probabilistic constraints**.","Both kinds of account could produce fast decoding."]}],"m3-reading1-p009":[{"text":"**Some pseudowords, like VONTH, PHANGE, STREAD and SOUGH, have more than one plausible pronunciation.**","children":["Each contains a spelling pattern English pronounces in more than one way.","Readers answer from spelling-sound patterns learned in familiar words.","Different familiar words favor different answers to the same item.","These items have no single agreed pronunciation.","They test how readers handle **ambiguity** in spelling-to-sound mapping.","So generalization can be **graded**, not all-or-none."]}],"m3-reading1-p010":[{"text":"**Readers accept both /strɛd/ and /strid/ for STREAD, which is hard for a rule set to cover.**","children":["**Generalization** is a good way to test what regularities people have learned about a system.","A new item cannot have been memorized, so the answer reveals the learned pattern.","The **Wug Test** is another generalization test.","The two readings of STREAD differ only in the vowel.","A **categorical rule** gives one output, so two acceptable outputs are hard for it.","A **statistical account** can give each pronunciation a different amount of support."]}],"m3-reading1-p011":[{"text":"**Irregular words are harder to recognize than regular words, which is the regularity effect.** Irregular words are also called **inconsistent**. Some form of decoding is uncontroversially part of normal reading.","children":["The effect is straightforward for PINT, ACHE and CHOIR.","Their usual spelling patterns give the wrong pronunciation.","It is less clear-cut for **neighborhoods** (sets of words sharing a spelling pattern).","ROLL, POLL and TOLL share one pronunciation, but DOLL differs.","COOK, TOOK and BOOK share one pronunciation, but SPOOK differs.","So difficulty depends partly on how spelling neighbors are pronounced."]}],"m3-reading1-p012":[{"text":"**Readers clearly use sublexical information, so the open question is how lexical processes work.**","children":["**Sublexical information** (information about units smaller than a whole word) is used especially to translate spelling to sound.","People read nonwords quickly and accurately.","Words with typical spelling-to-sound patterns are easier to read than words without them.","**Lexical information** (knowledge of particular words) also needs an account.","Nonwords test generalization. Differences among familiar words reveal word-specific knowledge."]}],"m3-reading1-p013":[{"text":"**Knowing a word helps identify an ambiguous letter inside it.** This comes from the **Interactive Activation Model** of **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**.","children":["Visual features activate letters.","Letters activate candidate words such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK.","Active words send **feedback** to their letters."]},{"text":"In a word perception example, blots cover parts of the letters in WORK.","children":["The last letter's visible features fit K and R, not D.","Only K makes a known word, so WORK feeds back to K.","**K** activation rises close to the maximum.","**R** activation rises a little, then falls back.","**D** activation stays low.","Recognition builds up over time, not at once."]}],"m3-reading1-p014":[{"text":"**In the Interactive Activation Model, known words feed activation back to their own letters.**","children":["**Feedback** from activation for known words increases activation for letters in those words.","Letter-to-word activation gives evidence for a word.","Word-to-letter feedback supports that word's letters.","So recognition is **interactive**, not only upward from features to words.","A word and its letters support each other.","So an ambiguous letter is easier to recognize inside a familiar word."]}],"m3-reading1-p015":[{"text":"**Real words are named faster than nonwords, and frequent words are named faster than rare ones.** These are two **lexical effects**.","children":["**Lexicality** compares whether the item is a word.","**Word frequency** compares how often a word has been met.","In one naming study, high-frequency words took about 442 ms and low-frequency words about 482 ms.","Nonwords took about 541 to 552 ms.","As with **picture naming**, past encounters speed naming.","**Balota and colleagues (2004)** used **English Lexicon Project** data.","Naming time drops steadily as frequency rises.","The effect is **robust** over a very large number of observations."]}],"m3-reading1-p016":[{"text":"**Frequency still predicts reading time after length and other variables are controlled.**","children":["A **confound** (another variable that changes with frequency) could fake a frequency effect.","Length was one worry, since frequent words could be shorter.","A **multiple regression analysis** (measuring each predictor's effect with the others included) tested this.","About 3000 words were read by 30 young and older adults.","The tasks were **lexical decision** (judging whether a string is a real word) and **naming**.","**Objective** and **subjective frequency** had **negative** coefficients in all four columns.","So higher frequency went with **faster** responses.","So the frequency effect is not just a confound."]}],"m3-reading1-p017":[{"text":"**The frequency effect is much larger for exception words than for regular words.** This **frequency by regularity interaction** is a landmark finding in psycholinguistics.","children":["**Exception words** have a pronunciation that breaks the usual spelling pattern.","**Naming latency** is the time to begin saying a word.","At **high frequency**, both kinds are named in about 540 ms.","At **low frequency**, exception words take about 583 ms and regular words about 557 ms.","An **interaction** means one factor's effect depends on the other.","So experience matters most when pronunciation conflicts with the usual pattern.","The finding has affected other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**."]}],"m3-reading1-p018":[{"text":"**One interpretation is that word recognition uses two separate processes, a sublexical one and a lexical one.**","children":["The **sublexical process** is decoding from spelling units.","The **lexical process** is knowledge of a particular word.","For **high-frequency (HF)** words, the lexical process is so fast that the sublexical process has little influence.","The sublexical process shows up only for **nonwords** and **low-frequency (LF)** words.","For an LF exception word, decoding gives a regularized answer.","That answer competes with the correct one, so naming slows.","This is one interpretation of the interaction, not the interaction itself."]}],"m3-reading1-p019":[{"text":"**Acquired phonological dyslexia is brain-injury reading difficulty specifically with pseudoword naming.** It is **acquired** (caused by injury) as opposed to **developmental** dyslexia.","children":["**Cacciopo van Vliet and colleagues (2003)** tested patients M.O. and I.B.","Both read real words and nonwords aloud.","**Real words** were about 85 to 100 percent correct.","This held across concreteness, word class and frequency.","**Nonwords** got plausible responses much less often, often under half.","Nonwords with low **grapheme-to-phoneme correspondence** were hardest, at about 15 to 20 percent.","A **grapheme** is a written unit standing for a sound.","So familiar-word reading survives while decoding new forms fails."]}],"m3-reading1-p020":[{"text":"**Acquired surface dyslexia is brain-injury reading difficulty specifically with irregular words.**","children":["Mispronunciations tend to be **regularizations** (reading a word by the common spelling-sound pattern).","DEAF becomes “deef”, SAID “sayed”, BROAD “brode” and SHOE “show”.","ONE becomes “own”, FLOOD “flude” and GONE “goan”.","STEAK becomes “steek”, SEW “sue” and SOOT “suit”."]},{"text":"Two patients, MP and KT, were tested.","children":["**Regular words** were about 89 to 100 percent correct.","**Nonwords** were about 95 to 100 percent correct.","**Exception words** dropped, most for low frequency.","KT scored 47 and 26 percent on high- and low-frequency exceptions. MP scored 93 and 73.","So the errors are systematic, not random."]}],"m3-reading1-p021":[{"text":"**Surface and phonological dyslexia show opposite patterns, which is a classic double dissociation.**","children":["**Surface dyslexia** impairs irregular words, with regularization errors.","Surface dyslexia leaves **pseudoword reading** relatively spared.","**Phonological dyslexia** impairs pseudoword naming.","Phonological dyslexia leaves **exception word reading** relatively spared.","A **double dissociation** (one group impaired on task A, another on task B) needs both directions.","Relatively spared does not mean perfect.","So the pair is hard to explain as one task being harder for everyone."]}],"m3-reading1-p022":[{"text":"**Double dissociations are powerful because the impairment reverses across patients.**","children":["If task A were simply harder, damage would hurt A more in every patient.","A patient with the reverse pattern rules that out.","In reading, phonological dyslexia hurts pseudowords more.","Surface dyslexia hurts exception words more.","So the two tasks place different demands on the reading system.","Each patient's profile is compared first, then the two profiles.","Two low scores without an opposite pattern are not a double dissociation.","A theory is still needed for what the separate processes are."]}],"m3-reading1-p023":[{"text":"**Double dissociations are evidence that pseudoword and irregular word reading rely on separable processes.** In **Morton's logogen model**, from **Morton and Patterson (1980)**, each dyslexia comes from different damage.","children":["**Logogens** (stored word units) come as visual input logogens and output logogens.","**Grapheme-phoneme conversion** turns letters into sounds without a stored word.","**Phonological dyslexia** results from specific damage to the grapheme-phoneme conversion module.","Nonwords have no stored word, so they are read poorly.","**Surface dyslexia** results from damage to input or output logogens, or their connections.","Exception words then rely on conversion, so they come out regularized.","These are theoretical components, not a brain map."]}],"m3-reading1-p024":[{"text":"**Patterson (1981) gave early hints that the dual-route account is not so simple.** In that account, words are read from stored whole-word pronunciations or by **non-lexical grapheme-phoneme translation**.","children":["Surface dyslexia errors were thought to use only the non-lexical route.","So they should show no lexical influences.","**Marcel (1980)** found several lexical influences in the error data.","**Neologisms** (made-up non-words) occurred less often than a purely non-lexical process predicts.","One patient read incense as “increase”, partly from the ambiguous consonant c.","A purely non-lexical error would have been the neologism “inkense”."]},{"text":"This poses a **classic dilemma**.","children":["Either surface dyslexia reflects a more complicated combination of the two routes.","Or non-lexical reading is itself a more complicated route.","Models of normal reading ask the same question, as in **Glushko (1979)**.","Patterson argued that combining normal and patient evidence holds the best promise."]}],"m3-reading2-p001":[],"m3-reading2-p002":[{"text":"**Dual-route models propose two routes because reading shows several findings that one route would not explain easily.**","children":["Pseudoword reading (reading pronounceable made-up words) is fast and automatic.","Word reading is faster.","Reading is fastest for **high-frequency** words, which are words that occur often in language.","Accuracy and response time are also affected by **regularity**, which is how consistently a spelling maps onto one pronunciation.","**Frequency and regularity interact**, so the effect of one depends on the other.","In **acquired dyslexia** (reading loss after brain damage), pseudoword reading and irregular word reading are **dissociable**, meaning one can be impaired without the other."]},{"text":"The model proposes two routes.","children":["The **sublexical route** does **grapheme-to-phoneme conversion**, which means turning written units into sounds. This is also called **decoding**.","The **lexical route** recognizes real words.","Two routes seem needed because dyslexia can impair one kind of reading without the other."]}],"m3-reading2-p003":[{"text":"**The Dual Route Cascaded model joins an interactive word route to a separate rule route.** Coltheart and colleagues (2001) published this model in Psych Review.","children":["Its lexical part is just the **Interactive Activation model**.","The model has **no implementation of semantics**, so it does not model meaning.","Its **orthographic lexicon** (store of written word forms) and **phonological lexicon** (store of spoken word forms) are both independent of semantics.","Its **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** has processing assumptions that are completely different from the interactive part."]},{"text":"Both routes can contribute activation toward a spoken response.","children":["The rule system joins the output instead of becoming another interactive layer.","So the DRC uses two different kinds of computation."]}],"m3-reading2-p004":[{"text":"**The Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System works serially, letter by letter, and frequency does not change how it works.**","children":["Its rules are activated **serially** (one after another), so it moves through the letters in order.","The rule system has **no feedback** from the lexical route.","Word frequency has no effect on it in any way.","The two routes meet only at the **phoneme response buffer** (the shared output stage), where the rule system is connected to the same output units.","Because they share those output units, the two routes can **compete** to activate different patterns."]}],"m3-reading2-p005":[{"text":"**Grapheme-phoneme conversion (GPC) rules map written units onto phonemes (sound categories), and a model also needs rules for choosing among them.**","children":["Some rules concern individual letters, while others concern several letters or a particular letter context.","**Context-sensitive rules** restrict a pronunciation to specified surrounding letters or positions.","**Meta-rules** decide when to apply rules at each level of analysis.","A **larger-unit rule** supersedes smaller-unit rules, so a rule for several letters takes priority over rules for its individual letters.","Position labels specify whether a mapping applies at the beginning, middle, end or all positions."]}],"m3-reading2-p006":[{"text":"**The GPC rules are often based on the most frequent pronunciation, but they work as fixed, categorical rules.**","children":["The rules follow the most frequent pronunciation for a letter or letter combination, so they are partly based on statistics.","The rule for **-OUGH** has weaker statistical support than the rule for **-EIGH**.","Even so, both count as rules and **behave the same way** during processing.","A **probabilistic account** (one where each alternative gets its own degree of support) would instead let different pronunciations carry different strengths of support.","A spread of pronunciation frequencies across many alternatives means more uncertainty than a single dominant pronunciation."]}],"m3-reading2-p007":[{"text":"**The DRC's simple rules miss many cases, and this was seen as a feature, not a bug.**","children":["Finer rules could pronounce more exceptions correctly, and the DRC's own rules went through several revisions to improve nonword reading.","The argument is that people do not use such finely tuned rules for the GPC route.","If they did, the **regularization errors** (pronouncing an exception word as if it followed the usual rule) seen in **surface dyslexia** would not appear.","The slowdown for low-frequency exception words would not appear either.","The theory claims that people's sublexical computation is constrained."]},{"text":"A **cognitively useful model** should reproduce the relevant failures, not only maximize pronunciation accuracy.","children":["The crucial test is whether the same processing assumptions produce the observed pattern of success, interference and regularization."]}],"m3-reading2-p008":[{"text":"**Informational encapsulation means that a process does not freely use every source of relevant knowledge.**","children":["Parsing strategies like **late closure** and **minimal attachment** were motivated by syntactic complexity alone.","The idea was that syntactic processing is informationally encapsulated from world knowledge and **lexical co-occurrence statistics** (how often words appear together).","The dual-route models are designed to be similarly modular.","The GPC route keeps running and gives correct pronunciations for many words, but it gives incorrect responses to some exceptions.","We can see its operation when people make **regularization errors** or when they are forced to name **pseudowords** (pronounceable made-up words)."]},{"text":"In dual-route accounts, GPC conversion proceeds without consulting whole-word meaning or lexical feedback.","children":["Pseudoword naming exposes the conversion procedure because no familiar lexical entry is available.","Regularization errors expose it when conversion favors the wrong pronunciation.","This resembles a modular parsing proposal, but the comparison concerns processing restrictions, not shared representations."]}],"m3-reading2-p009":[{"text":"**The DRC can simulate many phenomena in visual word recognition.**","children":["**Coltheart and colleagues (2001)** tested the model against effects including **frequency**, **lexicality** (whether an item is a word or a nonword), **regularity** and their **interactions**.","The tested effects also include **lexical decision** and patterns found in acquired dyslexia.","Accounting for several effects with one architecture is stronger than fitting a single result."]},{"text":"Evaluation also requires examining which assumptions stay fixed and which change between tasks.","children":["The model gives strong weight to human behavioral evidence as a constraint on its architecture.","The listed effects span reading aloud, lexical decision and patient performance.","These are related but distinct tasks, so any task-specific adjustment has to be identified as such."]}],"m3-reading2-p010":[{"text":"**To model lexical decision (deciding whether a written item is a word), the DRC adds decision criteria to its recognition activity.**","children":["This sometimes means going to extremes to make contact with the data.","The model first reduces **letter-word inhibition**, which makes the task harder. Without that change, the results make no sense.","A \"yes\" answer occurs if any single word has an activity above a threshold of 0.69.","A \"yes\" answer also occurs if the total activity over the orthographic input lexicon exceeds the threshold S of 10 for cycles 0 to 20. That threshold drops to 1.98 if lexicon activity exceeds 0.2 at cycle 20.","A \"no\" answer occurs if neither criterion is met by the deadline D of 42 cycles. The deadline becomes 48 if lexicon activity exceeds 0.112 after 20 cycles.","This is described as nearly twice as fast as the model normally reads aloud.","The thresholds and deadlines implement response rules, and their conceptual role matters more than the exact numbers."]},{"text":"The implementation also changes inhibition and uses time-dependent thresholds.","children":["These choices help the model fit the data.","They add assumptions beyond the basic two-route diagram."]}],"m3-reading2-p011":[{"text":"**The DRC's routes are modular, meaning each route has its own assumptions and limited contact with the other.**","children":["The GPC route and the lexical route each have their own processing assumptions.","They are **informationally encapsulated** from one another, so no lexical feedback reaches the GPC rules.","They are **domain-specific**, meaning the GPC rules take only letters as input and pass phoneme activations to the phoneme system.","Modularity has three related senses, namely restrictions on inputs, rules of computation, and restrictions on communication."]},{"text":"Modularity is a specific claim about how components operate and communicate.","children":["So it says more than that the model has two pathways."]}],"m3-reading2-p012":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989) treated reading skill as emerging from learned mappings among spelling, pronunciation and meaning.**","children":["They studied these mappings with **PDP models** (parallel distributed processing networks) and trained a simple associative network to learn them.","Orthography is spelling, phonology is sound structure, and semantics is meaning.","They studied mappings from orthography to phonology in a single route."]},{"text":"Reading skill emerges from learning rather than from a hand-built list of rules and lexical entries.","children":["A connection between domains represents a learned mapping, not an extra stored dictionary entry for each item."]}],"m3-reading2-p013":[{"text":"**In the PDP account, rules are generalizations learned from many words, not a separate process.**","children":["Whole-word knowledge is **distributed**, so some is stored in orthography-to-phonology mappings and some in other parts of the system.","The same learning mechanism can capture patterns shared by many words and information about one familiar word.","\"Distributed\" describes how knowledge is spread across the network, so the model still has memory for individual words.","The research strategy differs from dual-route models.","It asks whether **domain-general learning** (learning that is not specific to reading) can explain reading.","This contrasts with reverse-engineering the system from adult and neuropsychological data."]}],"m3-reading2-p014":[{"text":"**The network produces the frequency by regularity interaction without assuming that frequency and regularity come from different processes.**","children":["Regular words are consistent with statistical patterns from many other words.","Exception words are inconsistent with those patterns.","Frequency also affects how well words are learned, so highly frequent words are less affected by inconsistency with other words.","A low-frequency exception has both weak item-specific learning and poor support from the dominant pattern.","That combination creates a larger disadvantage than either factor alone."]},{"text":"The interaction is shared evidence that both architectures must explain.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p015":[{"text":"**Regularity is redefined as consistency, which depends on how many similar spellings support a pronunciation.**","children":["A **regular consistent** word, such as LATE or DUST, has many **friends** and no **enemies**. Friends are similar spellings with a compatible pronunciation, and enemies support a competing pronunciation.","A **regular inconsistent** word has many friends but at least one enemy, such as GAVE and MINT.","An **ambiguous** word has about equal numbers of friends and enemies, such as DOWN and KNOWN, or POUR and SOUR.","An **exception** has few if any friends and many enemies, such as HAVE and PINT."]},{"text":"Friends and enemies describe pronunciation relations among similar spellings, not semantic relations between words.","children":["Spelling-sound support varies in degree, and a simple regular or irregular label can hide that.","PINT has relatively little support compared with its competing neighborhood."]}],"m3-reading2-p016":[{"text":"**In principle, the network can pronounce nonwords using the same learned weights it uses for words.**","children":["A new spelling activates patterns that overlap with familiar spellings, so the network generalizes to it.","No stored entry for that particular nonword is required.","The same statistical patterns that give rise to regularity effects also support nonword reading.","This is proposed as a mechanism for decoding without a separate GPC rule module."]},{"text":"Novel pronunciations can come from applying learned statistical structure.","children":["Whether one learned mapping can handle both familiar words and new items must still be checked against the model's actual performance."]}],"m3-reading2-p017":[{"text":"**Early nonword accuracy was poor, which raises a question about the theory or the implementation.**","children":["Nonword accuracy was not very good in the early model.","This is a classic case of ambiguity in model evaluation.","One possibility is that the theoretical claim is wrong, so nonwords and exceptions cannot be read by the same associative mechanism.","The other possibility is that the problem lay in how the model was built. Its representations may fail to capture the similarities needed for generalization.","Improving the implementation while keeping the theoretical mechanism helps tell these possibilities apart."]},{"text":"A failed simulation does not identify its own cause.","children":["A representational improvement is a different theoretical move from adding a separate nonword route."]}],"m3-reading2-p018":[{"text":"**Nonword reading is related to phonological representations, meaning how sounds are coded in the model.**","children":["Many studies show a relationship between decoding (nonword reading) and phonological processing.","The early Seidenberg and McClelland model used **Wickelfeatures** (the early model's way of coding speech sounds), which were an unusual phonological representation.","**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations and got better nonword performance."]},{"text":"Changing the representation can improve decoding without adding a symbolic rule system.","children":["So at least part of the earlier nonword failure came from the phonological representation."]}],"m3-reading2-p019":[{"text":"**Generalization depends on similarity, so poor sound representations hurt generalization to new items.**","children":["This principle is inherited from behaviorism.","Poor phonological representations, like Wickelfeatures, fail to capture the similarity space of words in the language.","Word-specific information is more robust to poor representations, because it does not depend entirely on generalization.","A nonword has no item-specific experience, so it depends more heavily on generalization."]},{"text":"The same weakness can have unequal effects on words and nonwords without any dedicated nonword mechanism.","children":["Distinguishing a weaker shared representation from damage to a separate route matters, because the two locate the explanation differently."]}],"m3-reading2-p020":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999) manipulated the amount of noise in the phonological representations.**","children":["Increasing noise makes the sound representations less reliable while the general learning framework stays the same.","Adding noise produced poorer nonword decoding and relative sparing of word recognition.","The model replicated several key findings from the developmental dyslexia literature.","These included the link between the degree of phonological deficit and **phonological dyslexia** (a reading pattern tied to weak sound processing).","Noise is the manipulated property, so the simulation tests a directional mechanism rather than only observing a correlation."]},{"text":"A selective-looking reading deficit can emerge from degraded shared representations.","children":["Nonwords are especially dependent on reliable generalization, so they are especially affected."]}],"m3-reading2-p021":[{"text":"**Lesions to semantics produced a specific impairment on exception words.**","children":["Nonword reading can be specifically impaired even though the model has no dedicated nonword mechanism.","**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** explored the other end of this dissociation, **surface dyslexia** (a reading pattern with trouble on exception words).","They implemented a simplified semantic system in the **triangle model**, a network that links orthography, phonology and semantics.","An exception has weak support from typical spelling-sound regularities, while meaning-related support can help produce its learned pronunciation.","Reducing that support creates a surface-dyslexia-like pattern, without defining a separate exception-word list as the sole explanation.","A **lesion** here is a controlled change to the computational model, not an observed injury in a patient."]}],"m3-reading2-p022":[{"text":"**The triangle model is not simply the dual-route model renamed, because semantic representations are directly involved.**","children":["The dual-route model's lexicon is independent of semantics, while the triangle model's semantic representations take part directly.","A later model, **Harm and Seidenberg (2004)**, implemented **featural semantics** (meaning represented as features) and simulated the mapping of spelling to meaning.","The orthography-to-phonology (OP) and semantics-to-phonology (SP) mappings share the same processing assumptions and learning algorithm, and both are probabilistic.","These pathways interact over learning, so the OP pathway develops differently in the presence of the SP pathway.","This interactivity and gradedness lets the models capture that **double dissociations** (two impairments that each occur without the other) are never entirely clean.","Phonological dyslexics are not perfect on exception words, and surface dyslexics are not perfect on nonwords."]},{"text":"Multiple pathways alone do not make a model modular, because learning rules, information types and interactions set the accounts apart.","children":["The OP and SP mappings use the same general probabilistic assumptions, unlike the DRC's distinct GPC and lexical procedures."]}],"m3-reading2-p023":[{"text":"**Woollams and colleagues (2007) tested the prediction that semantic ability and exception-word reading are strongly associated.**","children":["The patients had **semantic dementia** (a progressive condition).","Lower semantic scores were associated with poorer low-frequency exception reading.","The model and patient results match in their overall relationships, although individual patients vary.","This links a reading weakness to a broader semantic measure, not only to a reading-task dissociation."]},{"text":"On the semantic score scale, values fall from left to right.","children":["The downward trend therefore means that poorer semantics goes with poorer exception reading.","It does not mean that better semantics harms reading."]}],"m3-reading2-p024":[{"text":"**Some patients fall outside the general trend, and Woollams and colleagues would treat them as outliers when evaluating model fit.**","children":["Patients who are left of the vertical boundary and above the horizontal boundary count as **statistical outliers** (scores that sit far from the general pattern).","This means the model misses a few data points, and those points are treated the way outliers are usually treated when judging model fit.","An outlier does not automatically refute the model, because an account of graded association does not predict zero variability.","The real question is whether these unusual cases form a stable separate pattern or arise within a noisy overall association.","Points like these should be judged by their location relative to the broader group of patients and by repeated measurements."]}],"m3-reading2-p025":[{"text":"**Following patients over time tests whether semantic and reading ability decline together.**","children":["Because semantic dementia is progressive, repeated testing can reveal a trajectory rather than a single unusual score.","In most cases, when a patient's semantic ability declined over time, the patient's reading ability also declined.","**Regression to the mean** (unusual scores moving back toward typical values on retesting) is the pattern that idiosyncratic outliers would show.","Observations from the same person are connected, so movement toward lower scores on both measures is coupled decline."]},{"text":"Repeated observations help test whether an apparent dissociation persists.","children":["A one-time exceptional score is less convincing if the patient's later scores follow the general relation."]}],"m3-reading2-p026":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues (2009) simulated surface dyslexia with the DRC by generating 40,000 model patients.**","children":["The model patients varied on two parameters, the integrity of the lexical route and the integrity of the GPC rules.","For each patient in the Woollams and colleagues data, the researchers selected the run of the model that best fit that patient's data.","This selection produced an excellent fit to the data.","The selection procedure is part of how that fit was achieved, so the fit differs from predicting each patient's pattern in advance."]},{"text":"The plotted patient and model lines lie close together.","children":["That closeness describes goodness of fit.","The large search over simulated patients describes flexibility."]}],"m3-reading2-p027":[{"text":"**A better fit can be less informative when it comes from much greater flexibility.**","children":["The question is why a better fit could count against this account.","Selecting the best match from many parameter combinations can reproduce a dataset without strongly constraining the explanation.","The issue is not that close agreement is bad in itself.","The issue is whether the account predicts the relation from principled assumptions or accommodates each case after seeing the outcome."]},{"text":"Goodness of fit is only one part of model evaluation.","children":["A model should explain why the pattern occurs, not only contain a setting that resembles it.","The contrast is between constrained prediction and flexible post hoc matching (fitting after the results are known)."]}],"m3-reading2-p028":[{"text":"**The dual-route and triangle models both have to explain the same basic reading patterns.**","children":["Both must explain rapid decoding of pseudowords (pronounceable made-up words), which is fast and automatic.","Words that diverge from typical spelling-to-sound mappings are harder to read.","This **regularity** (or **consistency**) effect interacts with word frequency.","Pseudoword reading and **exception word** reading can be dissociated in neuropsychological patients."]},{"text":"The models compete over explanation, not over whether these basic reading patterns exist.","children":["The models agree on the phenomena but differ on mechanisms.","One account emphasizes specialized routes, while the other emphasizes learning across interacting representational systems.","A useful comparison holds the phenomenon constant and asks what each model changes or computes to produce it."]}],"m3-reading2-p029":[{"text":"**The two models explain the same reading patterns with different mechanisms.**","children":["For pseudoword reading and regularity effects, the triangle model relies on statistical regularities learned over many words. The DRC instead relies on a dedicated grapheme-phoneme conversion rule system.","For frequency effects, the triangle model says more exposure to a word leads to better learning for that word.","The DRC instead uses distinct lexicons for input and output, separate from the GPC rules.","Word-specific exposure therefore strengthens learned mappings in the triangle account and affects lexical processing in the DRC."]},{"text":"For phonological dyslexia, the DRC points to deficit or damage in the GPC route. The triangle model points to deficit or damage in phonological processing generally.","children":["For surface dyslexia, the DRC points to damage in the lexical route, while the triangle model points to deficit or damage in semantic processes generally.","Each row gives competing explanations for the same finding, not two names for the same component."]}],"m3-reading2-p030":[{"text":"**The key contrast is whether explanations begin with separate reading routes or with broader language associations.**","children":["**Dual-route models** usually use **double dissociations** (two impairments that each occur without the other) to motivate independent processes.","Pseudowords and exception words can each be impaired on their own, so dual-route models treat them as independent processes.","Models informed by statistical learning reveal associations instead.","Phonology is important for learning and using OP (orthography-to-phonology) generalizations, while semantics is important for words where OP generalization is ambiguous."]},{"text":"Both dissociations and associations matter, because a theory should explain selective weaknesses and the related abilities that vary with them.","children":["The dyslexia rows of the comparison contrast GPC versus phonological damage and lexical route versus semantic damage.","These are the key theoretical alternatives, not just different diagnostic labels."]}],"m3-reading2-p031":[],"m3-morphology-p001":[],"m3-morphology-p002":[{"text":"**Morphology is generative, so speakers can form words they have never memorized.** Inflection in English mostly means the plural and the past tense.","children":["**Derivational morphology** (a process that turns one kind of word into another) once turned the verb \"to generate\" into the noun \"generative.\"","**Inflectional morphology** (marking grammatical information such as plural or past tense on a word) is the focus here.","Generalizing can produce playful forms, as in \"She found me crying and she crew too and we both crode.\"","Crew and crode extend irregular-sounding patterns to cry. They are creative, not standard English."]},{"text":"The open question is how speakers do this.","children":["One option is symbolic rules.","The other option is learning from patterns."]}],"m3-morphology-p003":[{"text":"**The wug test by Jean Berko Gleason (1958) found that people readily generalize inflection to new words.** Children do this too.","children":["A child sees one made-up creature with the text \"This is a WUG.\"","A second creature appears with \"Now there is another one. There are two of them.\"","The text ends with \"There are two\" and a blank.","The child supplies **WUGS**, a plural never heard before."]},{"text":"A new word rules out memory as the source of the answer.","children":["The child cannot have stored the plural of wug.","So the child must extend a learned pattern.","The same method tests the **past tense** with made-up verbs.","Success shows **productive knowledge** (the ability to apply a pattern to new items)."]}],"m3-morphology-p004":[{"text":"**The English past tense has regular forms that add -ed and irregular forms that do not.**","children":["**Regulars** with real verbs include show-showed, bake-baked and wade-waded.","**Regulars** with made-up verbs include blick-blicked, trog-trogged and wumble-wumbled.","**Irregulars** include go-went, take-took, eat-ate and sleep-slept."]},{"text":"The two kinds test different abilities.","children":["Made-up verbs test whether a pattern extends to new words.","Irregulars test whether exceptions are handled correctly.","Some irregulars share smaller patterns with other verbs."]},{"text":"The split echoes the **dual-route model** of reading.","children":["That model also separated a general pattern from word-specific knowledge."]}],"m3-morphology-p005":[{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues proposed that words and rules are separate processes with separate formats.** This is the **Words and Rules** account.","children":["**Regulars** use a **symbolic rule** (an abstract operation such as \"add -ed\").","**Irregulars** are **lexicalized** (stored in the word's own entry) within an **associative network**."]},{"text":"The rule needs no stored past tense.","children":["So the rule applies to new verbs."]},{"text":"The associative network holds word-specific information.","children":["Frequency and similarity influence it.","The account must also keep a stored irregular from being replaced by a regularization."]}],"m3-morphology-p006":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002) proposed a dual-route model with a lexicon route for stored forms and a grammar route for combining pieces.** The input is a stem such as walk or hold plus a past-tense feature.","children":["The **lexicon** retrieves held for hold by lookup and association.","It handles roots, idioms, irregulars and some regulars.","It belongs to **declarative memory** (words and facts) in temporo-parietal cortex.","The **grammar** combines walk with -ed by combination and unification.","It handles phrases, sentences and any regular form.","It belongs to the **procedural system** (rules and skills) in frontal cortex and basal ganglia.","A **blocking** link lets a retrieved irregular stop the regular form."]},{"text":"The model is **similar** to dual-route reading.","children":["Both separate word-specific knowledge from a productive operation."]},{"text":"The model is **different** in task and origin.","children":["Reading turns spelling into sound.","This model changes a stem according to a grammatical feature.","It came from grammar theory, not from patient data."]}],"m3-morphology-p007":[{"text":"**The past-tense model looks like the reading model but has different origins.**","children":["The reading model was built on neuropsychological **double dissociations** (two abilities that can each be damaged while the other is spared).","**Pinker and colleagues** were motivated by other concerns."]},{"text":"**Generative linguistics** of the time drove the past-tense model.","children":["It was mostly about **categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars**.","These rules often have **exceptions**.","The Words and Rules account keeps rules and exceptions apart."]},{"text":"The reading model came from patient data.","children":["The past-tense model came from a theory of grammar."]}],"m3-morphology-p008":[{"text":"**The dual-process model handles exceptions in a second process so the grammar can stay elegant.**","children":["Linguists often just left exceptions out of the grammar.","Adding machinery for exceptions would make the grammar **inelegant**.","The second process keeps the grammar **symbolic and categorical**."]},{"text":"The second process has all the properties the rule route lacks.","children":["It is **word-specific** (lexical).","It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence.","It is **sensitive to phonological similarity** (sharing sound patterns), as in keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt."]},{"text":"An exception can belong to a small family of similar verbs.","children":["The rule stays simple, but a second mechanism now needs explaining."]}],"m3-morphology-p009":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987) proposed that the past tense is all associative learning in one network.**","children":["No separate \"add -ed\" rule exists in the model.","Regularity is a pattern learned from many examples.","Irregulars are learned in the same system."]},{"text":"The model has three stages.","children":["A **fixed encoding network** turns the sound of the root form into **Wickelfeatures** (units that represent sound patterns).","A **pattern associator** with **modifiable connections** maps root Wickelfeatures onto past-tense Wickelfeatures.","A **decoding and binding network** turns those features into the sound of the past tense.","The modifiable connections store the learned mapping."]}],"m3-morphology-p010":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland aimed their model at U-shaped learning in children's past tense.** It was one of the first learning models of a language process, so it focused on development.","children":["First, children learn some frequent irregulars early, such as run-ran.","Then, spontaneously, they produce **regularizations** (applying -ed to an irregular verb), such as runned.","Eventually, they produce correct forms again."]},{"text":"The name comes from the shape of performance over time.","children":["Performance goes down.","Then performance goes back up.","Children get worse before they get better."]},{"text":"A theory must explain the whole path.","children":["That path includes the temporary errors."]}],"m3-morphology-p011":[{"text":"**In the Words and Rules account, U-shaped learning matters because each part of the U is a distinct stage with a distinct process.**","children":["Early correct forms are just **imitations** and do not use adult mechanisms.","When the rule is first learned, it is applied to all cases.","The rule is the **best hypothesis** for all items generally, so runned appears.","Over time, exceptions are learned **associatively**, one by one, into **lexical knowledge**.","The **blocking process** (a stored irregular stopping the regular form) also develops."]},{"text":"Blocking explains the recovery.","children":["Ran blocks runned.","The child is correct again."]},{"text":"The dip is rule use that exceptions do not yet hold back.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p012":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland's single network produces U-shaped learning when its input changes over time.** The **training set** is based on data about input to children.","children":["Early on, **irregular verbs** predominate.","The highest-frequency verbs tend to be irregular, such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","As vocabulary expands to more regulars, the model **reorganizes** to capture them.","This reorganizing **interferes** with some early-learned irregulars."]},{"text":"The model's curves follow the U.","children":["Correct features for high-frequency irregulars dip briefly, then climb slowly.","Regulars climb fast and stay high.","The regularized response briefly beats the correct irregular.","With training, the correct form wins and the regularized form fades."]}],"m3-morphology-p013":[{"text":"**Because the model was trained on few verbs, it could take a wug test with real words.**","children":["Untrained English verbs were new to the model.","They were unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs."]},{"text":"Many responses were correct regular forms.","children":["Examples are shaped, sipped and typed."]},{"text":"Some responses were odd.","children":["Examples are stepted for step, squawked for squat and membled for mail."]},{"text":"The proportion of **Wickelfeatures** generated correctly was high.","children":["It was .92 for regulars.","It was .84 for irregulars.","It was .91 overall.","Correct features do not guarantee correct whole words."]}],"m3-morphology-p014":[{"text":"**Some model outputs added or changed consonants in ways people would not.**","children":["The model gave maded for mate.","It gave squawked for squat.","It gave membled for mail.","It gave toureder for tour."]},{"text":"The model's authors admitted this problem.","children":["Humans are unlikely to make these errors, especially the last two.","Most of these responses were **near threshold** (only weakly produced).","An **auto-associative network** among output units could clean up the output and avoid bizarre responses.","They had not yet built that network."]},{"text":"A good learning curve is not enough if the words are implausible.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p015":[{"text":"**Prince and Pinker (1988) argued that the model blends competing past tenses into bizarre hybrids.**","children":["The model uses **feature-only representations** (representations made only of features).","With features alone, representing two things at once is a problem.","Features alone cannot tell a green circle near a red square from a red circle near a green square."]},{"text":"The same problem arises in the past tense.","children":["Subregularities and the regular rule all compete to activate Wickelfeatures for the past form.","For sing, features of sing, sang, sung, singed and sanged are superimposed.","The output is an unordered collection of features, many contradictory, not a single word."]},{"text":"The result is blended hybrids.","children":["Toureder is a hybrid past of tour.","Membled is a hybrid past of mail."]}],"m3-morphology-p016":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999) proposed that sound supports regular patterns while meaning gives extra support to irregulars.**","children":["**Rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations** (sound forms of words).","**Irregular** forms require greater support from **semantics** (word meaning)."]},{"text":"One system holds both kinds of information.","children":["**Speech input** and **semantics** connect through a middle layer to **speech output**.","No separate rule route exists."]},{"text":"Regulars and irregulars differ in how much they rely on sound and meaning.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p017":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999) aimed their model at a dissociation between irregular and nonword past tense in patients.**","children":["Some patients are worse on **irregulars**.","Other patients are worse on **nonwords**."]},{"text":"The model must produce both patterns from one system.","children":["Nonwords have no stored meaning, so they rely on phonological generalization.","Irregulars fit the main pattern poorly, so they rely more on semantics.","Damaging phonology or semantics should give opposite profiles."]},{"text":"The test is whether damage to shared systems explains both patient groups.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p018":[{"text":"**Broca's aphasia patients have problems with regular morphology, especially nonwords.**","children":["The cause is injury to **left anterior** brain regions.","Speech production is poor.","Nonword examples are wug becoming wugged and blick becoming blicked."]},{"text":"Accuracy follows a clear order.","children":["Irregulars are about 85 percent correct.","Regulars are about 82 percent correct.","Nonwords are about 69 percent correct."]},{"text":"The nonword drop is the largest.","children":["A nonword has no stored past tense.","It must be produced by generalizing.","This raises the question of a phonological cause."]}],"m3-morphology-p019":[{"text":"**Damaging phonology in the model reproduced the Broca's pattern, with nonwords worst.**","children":["The damage **reduced** the model's ability to compute past-tense forms."]},{"text":"**Nonwords** were worse than regulars and irregulars.","children":["Model nonwords were about 72 percent correct.","Patient nonwords were about 69 percent."]},{"text":"**Regulars** were numerically worse than irregulars.","children":["Model regulars were about 80 percent.","Model irregulars were about 85 percent."]},{"text":"Familiar words keep support a new nonword lacks.","children":["So weak sound processing hurts nonwords most.","No rule had to be removed."]}],"m3-morphology-p020":[{"text":"**Wernicke's, Alzheimer's and anomic patients show the reverse problem, with irregulars worst.**","children":["The anomic patients (people who have trouble retrieving words) have injury to **inferior or middle temporal** brain regions."]},{"text":"Accuracy follows the reverse order.","children":["Irregulars are about 60 percent correct.","Regulars are about 89 percent correct.","Nonwords are about 84 percent correct."]},{"text":"This is the other side of the dissociation.","children":["Broca's patients are worst on nonwords.","These patients are worst on irregulars."]}],"m3-morphology-p021":[{"text":"**Damaging semantics in the model made irregulars worst, as in Alzheimer's patients.**","children":["**Irregulars** were worse than regulars and nonwords.","**Nonwords** were a bit worse than regulars."]},{"text":"Model and patients matched on irregulars.","children":["Model irregulars were about 62 percent correct.","Patient irregulars were about 60 percent."]},{"text":"Model regulars were about 83 percent, and model nonwords about 77 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"Irregulars rely most on meaning.","children":["So losing semantic support hurts them most.","Typical sound patterns still support regulars and nonwords."]}],"m3-morphology-p022":[{"text":"**Two studies tied the patient dissociation to phonology and semantics rather than to rules and words.**","children":[]},{"text":"**Bird, Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, McClelland and Seidenberg (2003)** tested **Broca's patients**.","children":["They controlled regular and irregular items adequately for **phonology**.","The regular and irregular difference was **reduced**.","So part of the gap came from sound differences between items."]},{"text":"**Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, Hodges and McClelland (2001)** tested **semantic dementia** patients (people losing word meanings).","children":["They compared irregular impairment with severity of the semantic deficit.","Irregular impairment **correlated strongly** with semantic deficit severity.","So irregular trouble grows as meaning is lost."]}],"m3-morphology-p023":[{"text":"**As in the triangle model of dyslexia, phonology supports generalization and semantics supports idiosyncratic forms.**","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological representations** support **generalization**.","children":["Generalization comes from **statistical learning** about patterns from present to past.","Impaired phonological representations hurt these generalizations."]},{"text":"**Semantic representations** support **idiosyncratic** forms (forms particular to one word).","children":["Forms that deviate from the overwhelmingly regular \"add -ed\" pattern need semantic support.","Some irregulars use **sub-patterns** shared with similar verbs.","Those irregulars are likely to be **spared**."]}],"m3-morphology-p024":[{"text":"**Specific language impairment (SLI) is impaired language development with normal nonverbal IQ.** Performance outside language is \"normal.\"","children":["It affects grammar, in both **morphology** (word structure) and **syntax** (sentence structure).","It also affects **phonology** (sound structure).","It also affects **reading**.","It also affects **working memory**.","It also affects **speech perception**."]},{"text":"So the impairment reaches beyond grammar.","children":["A grammar-only theory must explain these too."]}],"m3-morphology-p025":[{"text":"**Earlier claims said SLI is a deficit in learning rules.** **Past-tense deficits** are a **sine qua non** (essential feature) of SLI.","children":["Gopnik, van der Lely, Pinker, Ullman and Clahsen made the claims.","A rule deficit predicts problems with **regulars**.","It predicts problems with **nonwords**.","It predicts no problem with **irregulars**, which are stored words."]},{"text":"If true, SLI would be evidence for **domain-specific** mechanisms (used only for language) in language acquisition.","children":["The test is whether irregulars are spared."]}],"m3-morphology-p026":[{"text":"**van der Lely and Ullman (2001) found that an SLI group was poor on all past-tense types, not only regulars and nonwords.**","children":["They compared the SLI group with **same-age controls**."]},{"text":"The SLI group was low on every type.","children":["Regulars were about 22 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 18 percent.","Nonwords were about 7 percent."]},{"text":"Controls were much higher on every type.","children":["Regulars were about 95 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 88 percent.","Nonwords were about 74 percent."]},{"text":"Irregulars were **not spared** in SLI.","children":["The deficit is broader than a rule deficit predicts.","Nonwords were still hardest."]}],"m3-morphology-p027":[{"text":"**A model with weakened phonology learned all past-tense forms poorly, and nonwords suffered most.**","children":["**Severe phonological deficit** gave about 39 percent for regulars, 17 for irregulars and 7 for nonwords.","**Weaker phonological deficit** gave about 55 percent for regulars, 38 for irregulars and 15 for nonwords.","**Intact, final** gave about 100 percent for regulars, 94 for irregulars and 80 for nonwords.","**Intact at 700K iterations** gave about 90 percent for regulars and 75 for both irregulars and nonwords."]},{"text":"The impaired models follow the SLI order.","children":["Regulars are best.","Nonwords are worst.","No rule is missing."]}],"m3-morphology-p028":[{"text":"**The model makes clear that phonology affects development of all past-tense forms and matters most for nonwords.**","children":["Every spoken past tense uses phonology.","Weak phonology lowers regulars, irregulars and nonwords."]},{"text":"Nonwords depend on phonology most.","children":["A nonword has no training history of its own.","It relies heavily on generalizing a sound pattern.","Familiar words get extra support from item-specific learning."]},{"text":"Nonwords show the largest deficit, but every form still depends on phonology.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p029":[{"text":"**In this account there are no special past-tense rules, only mappings between phonology and semantics.** It is similar to the **triangle model** of reading.","children":["There is no **separate lexicon** that stores exceptions.","**Rule** and **exception** only approximately describe the system."]},{"text":"Two associations support the account.","children":["**Irregular** forms are associated with **semantic processing**.","**Wug test** performance is associated with **phonological processing**."]},{"text":"Past-tense behavior comes from learned mappings that lean on sound and meaning to different degrees.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p030":[],"m3-thought-p001":[],"m3-thought-p002":[{"text":"**Linguistic relativity asks whether differences in language change how people organize concepts.** The quotations from **Sapir (1929)** and **Whorf (1956)** suggest that **linguistic categories** can shape how experience is interpreted.","children":["The strongest reading says language determines what a person can think.","A weaker reading says habitual language use biases attention, memory, or preferred ways of representing a situation."]},{"text":"**Influencing thought** is different from **completely determining thought**.","children":["The two readings need different evidence.","The quotations are broad ideas, not experiments."]}],"m3-thought-p003":[{"text":"**Language is embedded in cultural practices and experience.** A difference between language communities may go along with factors other than linguistic structure.","children":["Sapir and Whorf should not automatically get the strongest reading of their quotations.","In the first half of the twentieth century, linguistics was part of **anthropology**.","A precise study must separate the contributions of language and culture."]},{"text":"**Testing linguistic relativity needs a narrower claim.**","children":["**Domains** (specific areas of experience) such as space and time let researchers test preferred ways of representing things.","A study must identify which **cognitive process** (a mental operation such as remembering) is affected.","It must also check which alternative explanations the comparison controls."]}],"m3-thought-p004":[{"text":"**A location description needs a frame of reference.** A **frame of reference** is the set of directions used to say where something is.","children":["The ball can be located relative to the viewer.","The ball can be located relative to the chair's own orientation.","The ball can be located by a direction independent of both."]},{"text":"**Changing the reference changes the relation being described.**","children":["The object does not have to move for its description to change.","Different descriptions can identify the same physical position.","The question is which reference determines the ball's location."]}],"m3-thought-p005":[{"text":"**Each frame of reference fixes direction in a different way.** Three types are intrinsic, relative, and absolute.","children":["An **intrinsic frame** uses the object's own orientation.","A **relative frame** uses the observer's viewpoint.","An **absolute frame** uses a stable direction."]},{"text":"**The same location can be described in all three ways.**","children":["\"At the chair's front\" uses the chair's intrinsic front.","\"To the left of the chair from my viewpoint\" depends on the observer.","\"North of the chair\" does not reverse when the observer turns around."]},{"text":"**The ball and chair example keeps the scene fixed.**","children":["Rotating the observer changes left and right.","Rotating the observer does not change north.","This classification comes from **Levinson and colleagues (2002)**."]}],"m3-thought-p006":[{"text":"**Most languages describe nearby space egocentrically.** **Egocentric** descriptions use the speaker's own position and orientation.","children":["Left, right, in front, and behind are familiar examples.","These words describe nearby objects."]},{"text":"**Tzeltal commonly uses absolute directions instead.**","children":["Tzeltal is a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas.","A small number of other languages also use absolute directions.","Those languages are mostly unrelated and broadly distributed, such as in Bali and Queensland.","An absolute direction stays fixed when the person turns."]},{"text":"**The contrast raises a question about habits.**","children":["The question is whether habitual language use relates to nonverbal spatial coding.","The comparison concerns preferred descriptions."]}],"m3-thought-p007":[{"text":"**Tzeltal's uphill and downhill terms work as an absolute directional system.** The system was historically based on landmarks.","children":["Things are uphill or downhill from one another, not to the left or right of the speaker.","\"Uphill\" and \"downhill\" are used as cardinal directions."]},{"text":"**A landmark origin and an absolute use are different properties.**","children":["A term can come from a slope.","Each use does not simply point to the nearest visible hill."]},{"text":"**A stable reference keeps positions consistent.**","children":["The direction is not recalculated from the speaker's current left and right.","So positions can be described consistently when the speaker changes orientation."]}],"m3-thought-p008":[{"text":"**New Yorkers use uptown and downtown as if they were cardinal directions.** These terms work only inside Manhattan.","children":["So they are literally a landmark-based system.","Uptown and downtown organize movement within Manhattan.","They do not give a compass system that works everywhere."]},{"text":"**A local convention is not automatically an absolute frame.**","children":["Manhattan is angled with respect to magnetic north.","The subway map is off on north and south.","The Manhattan example is about reference systems, not causation."]}],"m3-thought-p009":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues (2004) used a non-linguistic test to separate relative and absolute coding of space.** The **chips task** rotates the participant 180 degrees.","children":["A participant sees a configuration, waits, and then chooses its match at a second table."]},{"text":"A **relative response** keeps the arrangement with respect to the person's viewpoint.","children":["An **absolute response** keeps the arrangement's orientation in the environment.","After rotation, the two responses predict different choices."]},{"text":"**Dutch speakers mostly gave relative responses.**","children":["**Tzeltal speakers gave more absolute responses.**","The north arrow stays fixed while the person changes orientation.","These patterns fit each group's preferred frame."]}],"m3-thought-p010":[{"text":"**Spatial inference can combine relations learned in different displays.** Participants learn a relation between two objects.","children":["They rotate to another display and learn a second relation.","That second relation involves a shared object.","They then place one object relative to another."]},{"text":"**Relative and absolute coding lead to different placements.**","children":["The person's orientation has changed between the displays.","The cube appears in both displays, linking the cone and cylinder relations.","The final placement separates a viewer-relative inference from one that keeps environmental direction."]},{"text":"**Dutch and Tzeltal patterns again differ.**","children":["Each pattern follows the direction linked to that group's preferred frame."]}],"m3-thought-p011":[{"text":"**Testing conditions may contribute to the apparent language difference.** **Li and Gleitman** noted that Tzeltal participants were tested outside, with visible, familiar landmarks.","children":["Typical psychology lab studies are done in windowless rooms.","Useful landmarks can encourage landmark-based coding."]},{"text":"**A language comparison can be confounded with a setting comparison.**","children":["The environment must be considered before assigning the whole difference to linguistic structure.","The critical contrast is access to spatial cues.","Windows, outdoor views, and familiar surroundings change which references are useful."]},{"text":"**Manipulating landmarks within English speakers tests this directly.**","children":["The test checks whether the behavior changes without changing native language."]}],"m3-thought-p012":[{"text":"**English speakers strongly preferred relative responses with the blinds down.** This matches the Dutch speakers in earlier studies.","children":["With the blinds up and a view of the street, the preference is less pronounced.","Outdoors, the preference is also less pronounced."]},{"text":"**Changing landmarks changes responses within one language group.**","children":["The language group stays the same while the spatial information changes.","So task context can influence the response.","This challenges an explanation in which language alone fixes the frame."]},{"text":"**The change is a weakening, not a full switch.**","children":["A reduced relative preference is not a complete move to absolute coding.","Describe the change without making it an all-or-none reversal."]}],"m3-thought-p013":[],"m3-thought-p014":[{"text":"**If language groups are tested under different conditions, their difference may partly reflect those conditions.** Visible landmarks and familiarity can go along with native language in a cross-community study.","children":["A controlled comparison must separate the environment from habitual linguistic coding."]},{"text":"**A confound is a factor that changes with the variable of interest.**","children":["It offers another explanation for the result.","It is not any feature that happens to be present."]},{"text":"**The bias might be an artifact of the way psychology tests are usually run in universities.**","children":["An **artifact** is a result produced by how tests are run.","So an uncontrolled comparison cannot separate language from testing conditions."]}],"m3-thought-p015":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues argued that the simplified task made the experimenter's purpose too obvious.** Low memory demands and short time or distance between displays make the rotation easy to notice.","children":["Li and Gleitman's participants were Ivy League students.","Ivy League students are good at working out what the experimenter wants."]},{"text":"**Participants who infer the purpose can change their answers.**","children":["That change reflects interpretation of the experiment.","It does not reflect habitual spatial coding.","This is a **demand-characteristic explanation** (an account where the apparent purpose of a study shapes responses)."]},{"text":"**The response focuses on memory load and task transparency.**","children":["It does not deny that landmarks can ever matter."]}],"m3-thought-p016":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues (2002) re-ran the animals in a row experiment with Dutch college students.** They added a fourth animal and a longer delay to raise memory load.","children":["They also increased the distance between the stimulus and response tables.","That larger distance reduces how obvious the rotation is."]},{"text":"**The bias toward relative framing remained under these conditions.**","children":["The preference for relative coding stayed in place.","The environmental effect depends on how the task is arranged."]},{"text":"**Demanding memory keeps the relative preference in place.**","children":["This narrows the conditions under which the landmark explanation accounts for behavior."]}],"m3-thought-p017":[{"text":"**Memory can make the interpretation of a relative preference harder.** Under memory demands, the preference could come from a verbal memory as well as a spatial one.","children":["Remembering the task can recruit verbal strategies as well as spatial ones.","The bias is robust when memory is significant and the experimenter's interest is not obvious."]},{"text":"**How the sequence is stored during the delay matters.**","children":["Researchers should ask how the sequence is stored, not only which direction is chosen.","A nonverbal response can follow verbal recoding inside the head."]},{"text":"**These two conditions say when the effect is robust.**","children":["Memory must be a significant part of the task, and the experimenter's interest in rotation must not be obvious."]}],"m3-thought-p018":[{"text":"**Participants might remember the animals as a verbal list.** An example list is \"lion, tiger, bear.\"","children":["Participants could reconstruct that sequence from left to right.","That reconstruction can produce a response that looks relative."]},{"text":"**The strategy connects language to the task through working memory.**","children":["The same choice can arise from different internal codes."]},{"text":"**A list keeps order but not compass direction.**","children":["Translating that order into a left-to-right display can mimic a spatial frame preference.","A later page links reading direction to how sequences are arranged in space."]}],"m3-thought-p019":[{"text":"**Metaphorical language expresses one kind of experience using terms from another.** Calling an argument ineffective describes how well it works.","children":["It is not a literal action with a blade.","Physical and spatial terms can help express abstract relations."]},{"text":"**An expression can be meaningful without being literal.**","children":["Time is useful for studying whether such habits go with nonlinguistic representations."]},{"text":"**A lyric from Brown and Byrd (1970) is one example of metaphor.**","children":["It raises the question of how much of language can safely be described as non-metaphorical."]}],"m3-thought-p020":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010) compared spatial metaphors for time in English and Mandarin.** English and Mandarin both use horizontal spatial language for time.","children":["Mandarin also often uses vertical terms.","An earlier event can take an \"up\" term, and a later event can take a \"down\" term."]},{"text":"**These expressions are the starting observation.**","children":["A behavioral task is needed to test whether the vertical contrast goes beyond words."]},{"text":"**Habitual talk about time suggests testable predictions about processing.**","children":["The \"up\" in an earlier month is a temporal use.","It does not claim that the month occupies a physical location."]}],"m3-thought-p021":[{"text":"**Boroditsky (2001) ran a priming experiment on temporal judgments.** Participants first process a horizontal or vertical spatial relation.","children":["They then answer whether one event is earlier or later.","English speakers respond faster after horizontal primes than after vertical primes.","Mandarin speakers have the opposite pattern in the comparison tested."]},{"text":"**Short training on vertical metaphors for time changes the pattern.**","children":["This suggests sensitivity to experience.","It is not an unchangeable split between language groups."]},{"text":"**Reaction time differences reveal how compatible the prime is with the judgment.**","children":["Lower reaction times mean faster processing.","So horizontal and vertical results should be compared within each group."]}],"m3-thought-p022":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2005) offer reading direction as an alternative explanation.** Reading and writing habits can also organize sequences spatially.","children":["Differences attributed to temporal metaphors may go along with whether text is read left to right, right to left, or vertically.","Casasanto and Bottini use differences in text orientation and arrangement preferences to support this."]},{"text":"**Language groups can bundle linguistic and literacy experience together.**","children":["Reading direction must be measured or controlled.","The diagrams separate the starting side from the line direction.","The table lists several arrangement patterns within the groups."]},{"text":"**Individual experience matters.**","children":["A writing system label does not perfectly predict each person's habits."]}],"m3-thought-p023":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010) excluded Chinese readers who reported reading top to bottom.** This tests whether the vertical effect is explained entirely by that reading experience.","children":["English speakers have an advantage for a left-is-earlier horizontal mapping.","Mandarin speakers have an advantage for a top-is-earlier vertical mapping in the comparison tested."]},{"text":"**A vertical effect that remains weakens that particular explanation.**","children":["A targeted control tests one specific alternative."]},{"text":"**Lower reaction times mean a mapping advantage.**","children":["Canonical mappings are the white bars.","Noncanonical mappings are the black bars.","For vertical trials, canonical means top is earlier."]}],"m3-thought-p024":[{"text":"**Participants sort events as past or future by pressing colored buttons.** The mapping from color to category is reversed across blocks.","children":["So the past response is on the left half the time and on the right the other half."]},{"text":"**Responses are faster when past maps left and future maps right.**","children":["**Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** used standard text reading for this comparison.","Color alone cannot explain the pattern, since the mappings are **counterbalanced** (the two mappings are used in turn)."]},{"text":"**The effect concerns response time across mappings.**","children":["It does not change which events count as past or future.","The left to right temporal mapping has the lower reaction times."]}],"m3-thought-p025":[{"text":"**Short-term reading experience can change the link between space and time.** **Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** had participants read reversed text, then perform the past and future button task.","children":["By the second mirror-reading block, the compatibility effect reverses.","Responses are then faster when earlier maps right and later maps left."]},{"text":"**A flexible temporal mapping is hard to explain as a fixed result of grammar alone.**","children":["Recent perceptual and reading experience contributes to performance.","The first block keeps the usual advantage.","The second block favors the opposite mapping."]},{"text":"**Compare the two colors within each block.**","children":["Comparing overall reaction times between blocks can mislead."]}],"m3-thought-p026":[{"text":"**Claims about a language's vocabulary are not automatic evidence for cognitive limits.** **Pullum (1989)** criticized how an account of different word roots became an exaggerated story about language and thought.","children":["Languages can express distinctions through separate roots, derived words, or phrases.","Counting one form without the others can misrepresent expressive capacity."]},{"text":"**A reliable claim needs accurate linguistic description.**","children":["It also needs an appropriate behavioral test.","Repeating a striking anecdote is not enough."]},{"text":"**Languages can package the same distinctions in different ways.**","children":["**Derivationally related forms** are words built from another word.","Roots, derived forms, and phrases differ, which is why simple counts mislead."]}],"m3-thought-p027":[],"m3-modalities-p001":[],"m3-modalities-p002":[{"text":"**Sign language and Braille are really different cases, but both reveal how the brain organizes language input from different senses.**","children":["A **signed language** is a **natural language** (a language with its own linguistic structure).","**Braille** is a system that lets blind people read a spoken language they share with sighted people.","So Braille is a way to read a language, not a separate language."]},{"text":"They are still grouped together, because both reveal the **plasticity** (the ability of sensory systems to change with experience).","children":["Both show how the brain gets organized differently when language input comes through a different **modality** (the sense that carries the input).","The linguistic status of the two cases still differs."]}],"m3-modalities-p003":[{"text":"**Sign languages differ from spoken languages in modality, articulators, use of space and iconicity.**","children":["**Emmorey and colleagues (2004)** list these differences.","**Modality** is the sense the language uses, which is vision for sign.","**Articulators** are the body parts that produce the language, which are the hands, face and upper body.","**Use of space** matters, because where a sign is made can carry relations between things.","**Iconicity** means a sign's form resembles its meaning, and some signs are iconic."]},{"text":"Iconicity is **easy to exaggerate**.","children":["It looks strongest when a single sign is studied with its **gloss** (the translation word for that sign).","A resemblance looks obvious only after the translation is known.","A learner still has to learn the forms and structure of the language."]}],"m3-modalities-p004":[{"text":"**Fluent signing coordinates the hands, face, upper body and eye contact in one continuous stream.**","children":["**Eye contact** is part of the interaction.","The **hands**, **face** and **upper body** work together as one linguistic signal.","The signal is spread across all of these articulators, not produced by one hand movement at a time."]},{"text":"Fluent signing is also **fast** and **fluid**.","children":["Signs run into each other, so it is hard to find where one word ends.","A learner may struggle to **segment** the stream (find the boundaries between words).","Fluent speech has the same problem, because it is also continuous."]},{"text":"The thing to study is coordinated language production, not one isolated hand pose.","children":["A video of fluent signing is available to watch for this point."]}],"m3-modalities-p005":[{"text":"**Signs can differ contrastively in hand configuration, location and movement, as speech sounds can differ contrastively.**","children":["**Emmorey and colleagues (2003)** name these three features.","**Hand configuration** is the shape of the hand.","**Location** is where the gesture is made.","**Movement** is how the hand moves."]},{"text":"The signs for \"please\" and \"sorry\" differ, and so do the signs for \"onion\" and \"apple.\"","children":["Changing one feature can turn one sign into a different sign.","Visual similarity alone does not decide whether two forms belong to the same category."]},{"text":"A feature is **contrastive** when changing it changes the sign's identity.","children":["The pairs of signs share some properties and contrast on others.","Handshape, the location of the gesture and its movement are separate dimensions to check.","Sign has its own **phonology** (the system of contrastive form that separates signs)."]}],"m3-modalities-p006":[{"text":"**Researchers asked whether categorical perception applies to these sign features.**","children":["**Categorical perception** (a pattern where a gradual change is sorted into separate categories) can be tested by changing a sign feature in steps.","Observers identify each form and also tell pairs of forms apart.","One continuum crosses a **phonemic** contrast (a difference that changes which sign is meant), between \"please\" and \"sorry.\"","The other continuum changes hand configuration without changing the sign's identity, which is an **allophonic** change."]},{"text":"Comparing equal steps is more informative than comparing only the endpoints.","children":["A step that crosses a linguistic boundary can be compared with an equal step inside one category.","Both continua vary visually, but only one crosses the relevant contrast."]},{"text":"The results show categorical perception for the phonemic change but not for the allophonic change.","children":["A sharp identification boundary with better discrimination near it would indicate category-sensitive perception."]}],"m3-modalities-p007":[{"text":"**Deaf signers show a sharper categorical boundary for the phonemic change than for the allophonic change.**","children":["For the phonemic change, **identification** (labeling each form as one sign or the other) shifts steeply between stimuli 4 and 6.","That steep change marks the boundary between the two categories."]},{"text":"**Discrimination** (telling two forms apart) peaks at that same boundary.","children":["Pairs of forms that cross the boundary are easier to tell apart.","This matches the peak in the discrimination function."]},{"text":"The allophonic change looks very different.","children":["Identification is much shallower, so many more forms are ambiguous.","There is no peak in discrimination.","The perceptual pattern follows the linguistic contrast, not the amount of visible change."]},{"text":"**A sharp identification shift and a discrimination peak together support categorical perception.**","children":["Identification alone would be a less complete test."]}],"m3-modalities-p008":[{"text":"**Sign language uses space to indicate relationships between words in a sentence.**","children":["A **classifier sign** (a sign whose form stands for a kind of object) is made at a location.","The relative position of two objects is given by where that classifier sign is made.","This example is very transparent."]},{"text":"**Understanding sentences of any complexity in sign requires keeping track of where signs were made.**","children":["Each entity introduced in space has to be remembered at its location.","Later signs relate back to those locations.","So spatial processing contributes to comprehension, and it is not only background vision."]}],"m3-modalities-p009":[{"text":"**Brain activity during sign comprehension is similar to spoken language in some ways and different in others.**","children":["Signing is **left lateralized** (its activity is concentrated in the left hemisphere), as spoken language is.","Classical language areas are active too, including **Broca's area** (the inferior frontal region) and **Wernicke's area** (the posterior superior temporal region)."]},{"text":"Signing also differs.","children":["It engages **visual areas** and the **superior parietal** region, which are classic spatial areas.","This is because signing makes perceptual and spatial demands."]},{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people who watched audio-visual speech.","children":["Hearing signers are useful, because signing experience is not limited to deaf people.","Overlap in these regions does not require identical activation everywhere."]}],"m3-modalities-p010":[{"text":"**Bavelier and colleagues (2001) asked whether deafness or experience with sign changes spatial attention.**","children":["The groups were hearing controls, deaf signers and hearing signers.","Participants monitored for brief changes in **luminance** (brightness) or **velocity** (speed of motion) of dots.","Attention was directed either to the center of the screen or to the periphery."]},{"text":"**Deaf participants had an advantage when attention was directed to the periphery.**","children":["Hearing signers did not show that advantage.","So the enhancement goes with deafness, not with signing experience."]},{"text":"Including hearing signers helps separate sign experience from the lack of auditory input.","children":["A deaf versus hearing comparison alone would bundle deafness and signing together.","If all signers had the advantage, signing alone would be the stronger explanation.","The task measures attention to brief visual changes, not sign comprehension."]}],"m3-modalities-p011":[{"text":"**Learning sign language and being deaf have different effects on visual processing.**","children":["Learning sign language moves the **laterality** (which side of the brain does the work) of motion perception from right to left.","Both deaf and hearing signers show this leftward shift compared with hearing nonsigners."]},{"text":"**Enhanced peripheral attention is found only in deaf individuals.**","children":["Hearing signers do not show this enhancement.","One proposed reason is that hearing people depend more on auditory cues when things are at the far periphery."]},{"text":"One proposed reason for the leftward shift is that motion perception needs to talk to left-lateralized language networks in people who learn sign.","children":["These two effects should not be merged into one advantage.","They track different aspects of experience, one in motion areas and one in center-versus-periphery attention."]}],"m3-modalities-p012":[{"text":"**Comparing sign with tic-tac separates language processing from the processing of hand movements.**","children":["**Tic-tac** is an outdated gesture system used to communicate about horse-racing odds in the UK.","Both tasks involve meaningful, sequenced hand movements.","So the control shares movement demands with sign but differs in language."]},{"text":"The control is not a natural language, and that is why the remaining difference is informative.","children":["A difference between sign and tic-tac says more about language than a comparison with no movement.","The comparison does not assume that every meaningful gesture is a natural language."]}],"m3-modalities-p013":[{"text":"**Sign comprehension produces stronger activity around Wernicke's area than tic-tac does.**","children":["**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** reported this comparison.","Wernicke's area lies in the posterior superior temporal region.","This supports a difference between processing organized hand movements and processing a natural language."]},{"text":"Shared visual movement does not explain the whole neural response to signing.","children":["The frontal activation is above **Broca's area**, so it should not be called a precise Broca's effect.","The posterior temporal difference is the central language-related result.","The comparison weakens a simple explanation based only on hands moving through space."]}],"m3-modalities-p014":[{"text":"**Signed languages have phonological structure, and native speakers show categorical perception for some contrastive features.**","children":["Learning a signed language causes some brain reorganization, especially in the lateralization of motion processing.","Attention to the visual periphery matters for both hearing and deaf signers.","Only deaf signers show enhanced peripheral attention."]},{"text":"Compared with other organized hand movements that are not language, signed sentences activate the **posterior superior temporal gyrus** (Wernicke's area).","children":["This region is a language-related region, so the response is not only about hand movement."]},{"text":"**A full account has to separate shared language organization from sensory adaptation.**","children":["Neither \"sign is just gesture\" nor \"modality makes no difference\" fits the evidence.","Each result answers a different question, so one result cannot establish the others."]}],"m3-modalities-p015":[{"text":"**Comprehending a signed spatial description can require a perspective transformation.**","children":["A room can be described from the **speaker's perspective** (the signer's own viewpoint) or the **addressee's perspective** (the viewpoint of the person being addressed).","Comprehension was better for descriptions from the speaker's perspective.","That is true even though it requires a mental transformation."]},{"text":"The most natural representation is not always the one with no spatial transformation.","children":["Spatial comprehension depends on conventions as well as geometric convenience.","The two signing sequences describe the same table location.","The difference is whose perspective organizes the description, not where the table is."]}],"m3-modalities-p016":[{"text":"**Braille reading is in many ways like reading print, but its symbols are built for touch.**","children":["**Braille symbols** are optimized for **tactile discrimination** (telling symbols apart by touch).","Reading Braille by sight is really hard.","Fingers take in letters one at a time."]},{"text":"Braille represents written language through touch.","children":["It is a way to read a language, rather than a separate natural language with its own grammar.","This makes it useful for asking how reading networks work when the input comes through touch."]},{"text":"**Sequential tactile sampling** is a processing constraint, because the fingers take in the letters in sequence.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p017":[{"text":"**Reading Braille activates visual cortex in blind people, and more visual cortex is recruited after earlier sight loss.**","children":["The comparison includes **early-blind** and **late-blind** participants.","Early-blind participants show stronger involvement of the **visual word form area** (a region involved in visual word processing).","The earlier sight is lost, the more extensive the visual activity to tactile stimuli."]},{"text":"A region's usual sensory label does not completely determine the information it can help process.","children":["Visual cortex can take part in reading even when the input is touch.","Early- and late-blind groups give a comparison of developmental sensory history."]},{"text":"Compare how much activation there is, rather than assuming that every colored spot has the same function.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p018":[{"text":"**Sighted adults who wore blindfolds for five days learned Braille more quickly than a control group.**","children":["**Merabet and colleagues (2008)** asked sighted adults to wear blindfolds for five days while they trained to read Braille.","The control group trained without the same continuous removal of visual input.","This suggests that removing competing visual input can affect tactile learning over a short time."]},{"text":"The study tests a question about **competition** (inputs competing for the same brain areas).","children":["The question was whether competition could block hearing people from building strong peripheral attention.","Because the participants were sighted adults, the study tests a different question from lifelong or early blindness."]},{"text":"**Cross-modal changes do not have to be limited to early development.**","children":["A short-term change can reveal **latent** (hidden) contributions to tactile processing.","The procedure included baseline testing, training, repeated imaging and blindfold removal.","Comparing the groups separates the effect of training from the extra effect of continuous blindfolding."]}],"m3-modalities-p019":[{"text":"**Visual cortex responses to Braille developed during blindfolding and reversed after the blindfold was removed on Day 6.**","children":["Over the course of the experiment, sighted blindfolded participants began to show activity in **primary visual cortex** (the first cortical area for vision). They showed this activity while reading Braille.","The change was rapid and reversible."]},{"text":"Removing visual input lets tactile-related activity become more evident.","children":["This pattern fits **unmasking** (existing inputs becoming visible) rather than building a new pathway in a few days.","Restoring vision shifts that balance again."]},{"text":"**Day 6 is the post-blindfold condition, not another day of blindfolding.**","children":["The comparison covers Days 1, 3, 5 and 6."]}],"m3-modalities-p020":[{"text":"**Disrupting visual cortex with TMS (transcranial magnetic stimulation, a method that briefly interferes with brain activity) impaired Braille reading only in the blindfolded group.**","children":["TMS was used to disrupt activity in visual cortex.","On Day 5, the blindfolded group's error rate rose under 1 Hz TMS compared with its baseline and with sham (fake) stimulation.","The nonblindfolded group did not show the same selective disruption.","Solid symbols mark the blindfolded participants."]},{"text":"The vertical axis is error rate, so an upward change means worse performance.","children":["**Activation and necessity are different kinds of evidence.**","Interfering with a region and changing behavior is stronger evidence that the region contributes to the task.","The effect is no longer present in the same way after blindfold removal, which supports a temporary contribution of visual cortex to tactile performance."]},{"text":"**One proposed explanation is that visual cortex always receives weak tactile input.**","children":["That input is too weak to compete with the much stronger and more consistent visual input.","So a short removal of visual input can unmask tactile responses in visual cortex."]}],"m3-modalities-p021":[{"text":"**Language circuitry stays resilient across sensory modalities, but experience still shapes how it is organized.**","children":["Classical language areas are active for sign, even though the input is dramatically different in its surface form.","Braille reading seems to engage networks that are important for processing text."]},{"text":"**Brain organization is shaped over development by competition between inputs.**","children":["When auditory input is unavailable, putative (assumed) auditory areas are used to process sign.","Visual attention is enhanced at the periphery.","When an input is absent or reduced, other information can make a larger contribution."]},{"text":"The competing processes that shape language are also important in the Competition Model for second language learning.","children":["Distinguish the language-network overlap from the visual-attention and tactile-recruitment findings, since they come from different experimental comparisons."]}],"m3-review-p001":[],"m3-review-p002":[{"text":"**Scripts differ in how regularly their spelling matches their sounds, and English is a relatively deep script.** This difference is called **orthographic depth** (how consistently spelling corresponds to pronunciation).","children":["A **deep** script has many irregular spellings.","A **shallow** script has regular letter-sound matches."]},{"text":"The script types line up as follows.","children":["**English** is relatively deep, due to its irregularities.","**Finnish** and **Korean** are very shallow.","**Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are relatively regular."]},{"text":"A **\"mispronouncing contest\"** made the point.","children":["People willfully mispronounced COLONEL and PARADIGM by following their letters.","**Phonemes are not letters.**","**Letters are not phonemes** either."]}],"m3-review-p003":[{"text":"**Finnish spelling is so transparent that children learn to decode it very quickly.** The **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis** calls a transparent script **shallow**.","children":["Each letter corresponds more or less unambiguously to one **phoneme** (a sound category of the language).","**Doubled consonants** stand for **geminated** (held longer) consonants.","**Doubled vowels** stand for long vowels."]},{"text":"Following a beginning Finnish lesson with its text makes the system clear.","children":["After about a minute, a listener gets the idea."]},{"text":"**Decoding** (producing a pronunciation from a written form) is easy to learn here.","children":["One letter-sound match works across many words."]}],"m3-review-p004":[{"text":"**Children learn to decode more slowly in English than in most other alphabetic languages.** English is an **outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet.","children":[]},{"text":"The test uses **pseudowords** (made-up words that follow a language's spelling and sound patterns).","children":["A pseudoword cannot be read from memory.","It must be decoded from letter-sound patterns."]},{"text":"The comparison is children in **Grade 1**.","children":["Children learning many other languages give plausible pseudoword pronunciations at a **much higher rate**.","Children learning English give them at a lower rate.","English spelling is less predictable, so the patterns are harder to use."]}],"m3-review-p005":[{"text":"**Korean writing is even more regular than Finnish, because similar sounds get similar symbols.** The letters sit in **syllable blocks** with initial, medial and sometimes final positions.","children":["The symbols for **similar phonemes** look similar to one another.","So a letter's shape reflects how its sound is made."]},{"text":"**Velar place of articulation** (made with the back of the tongue at the soft palate) has a shared written feature.","children":["The velar consonants share the angular shape **ㄱ**.","Doubling the shape or adding a stroke separates **g**, **kk** and **k**."]}],"m3-review-p006":[{"text":"**The frequency effect in naming is much larger for exception words than for regular words.** This landmark finding is the **frequency by regularity interaction**.","children":["**Naming latency** (time to start saying a written word) is the measure.","A **regular word** follows the usual spelling-sound pattern. An **exception word** does not."]},{"text":"The two frequency levels give different results.","children":["**High-frequency** exception and regular words are both named in about 540 ms.","**Low-frequency** exception words take about 580 ms.","**Low-frequency** regular words take only about 555 ms."]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other.","children":["Being an exception costs time mainly when a word is rare.","The finding has impacted other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**."]}],"m3-review-p007":[{"text":"**One interpretation says reading uses two separate processes, and only rare words and nonwords reveal the slower one.**","children":["The **sublexical process** is decoding from spelling units.","The **lexical process** is knowledge of a particular word."]},{"text":"Each word type relies on the processes differently.","children":["**High-frequency (HF) words** use a lexical process so fast that decoding has little influence.","**Low-frequency (LF) words** use a slower lexical process, so decoding shows up.","**Nonwords** have no stored entry, so they depend on decoding."]},{"text":"For a low-frequency exception, decoding gives a regularized pronunciation.","children":["That wrong output competes with the real one.","So low-frequency exceptions are slow."]}],"m3-review-p008":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland's (1989) PDP model explains reading with general learning instead of separate rules and a separate lexicon.** A **PDP model** is a network that learns connections between spelling and sound.","children":[]},{"text":"Its assumptions differ from dual-route models.","children":["**Rules** are just generalizations from lots of words, not a separate process.","The **lexicon** is distributed.","Some whole-word information sits in the **O to P mappings** (orthography to phonology).","Some sits in other parts of the system."]},{"text":"Its research strategy also differs.","children":["Dual-route models reverse-engineer reading from adult and **neuropsychological** data.","The PDP approach uses **domain-general learning mechanisms** (learning not specific to reading)."]}],"m3-review-p009":[{"text":"**The PDP model produced the frequency by regularity interaction from one learning process.** It did not assume that frequency and regularity come from different processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Each factor works through learning.","children":["**Regular words** are consistent with statistical patterns from many other words.","**Exception words** are inconsistent with these statistics.","**Frequency** affects how well a word is learned."]},{"text":"The factors combine.","children":["**High-frequency** words are learned well, so inconsistency affects them less.","**Low-frequency exceptions** get weak learning and no support from other words.","So low-frequency exceptions are the hardest."]}],"m3-review-p010":[{"text":"**In a PDP model, nonwords are read through the same route as words.** A **nonword** is a made-up letter string with no stored entry.","children":["Nonwords are named with the **same set of weights** (learned connection strengths) used for words.","No separate rule system is needed."]},{"text":"**Generalization** uses the same **statistical patterns** that create regularity effects.","children":["Regular words benefit from patterns shared with many words.","New nonwords are pronounced by applying those same patterns."]}],"m3-review-p011":[{"text":"**The Seidenberg and McClelland model read nonwords poorly, and the cause was unclear.** This is a classic case of **ambiguity in model evaluation**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two causes are possible.","children":["The **theoretical claim** might be wrong. The claim is that nonwords and exceptions use the same associative mechanism.","Or the problem might be specific to **how the model was made**."]},{"text":"Repairing the model decides between them.","children":["A better implementation that reads nonwords well keeps the theory alive.","Later work improved the **phonological representations** (how the model codes speech sounds)."]}],"m3-review-p012":[{"text":"**Better phonological representations made a later PDP model much better at reading nonwords.** Many studies relate **decoding** (nonword reading) to **phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first model used odd representations.","children":["**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** coded sounds as **Wickelfeatures**.","Wickelfeatures captured sound similarity poorly.","So nonword reading suffered."]},{"text":"The later model fixed the representations.","children":["**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations.","Their model produced **better nonword performance**.","No separate rule system was added."]}],"m3-review-p013":[{"text":"**Poor sound coding hurts nonwords more than words, because nonwords depend entirely on generalization.** **Generalization depends on similarity**, a principle inherited from **behaviorism**.","children":[]},{"text":"Wickelfeatures were a weak code.","children":["They failed to capture the **similarity space** (how words in the language relate in sound).","So learning transferred poorly to new forms."]},{"text":"Words and nonwords fare differently.","children":["**Familiar words** have **word-specific information** from their own training.","That information is **more robust** to poor representations.","**Nonwords** have no word-specific information.","So nonwords suffer most."]}],"m3-review-p014":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999) added noise to a model's phonology and reproduced the pattern of developmental dyslexia.** **Developmental dyslexia** is a reading impairment that appears during development.","children":[]},{"text":"They manipulated the amount of **noise** in the phonological representations.","children":["More noise means less reliable sound codes.","The rest of the model stayed the same."]},{"text":"The noisy models replicated two key findings.","children":["A bigger **phonological deficit** went with more **phonological dyslexia** (poor nonword decoding).","**Word recognition** was relatively spared."]}],"m3-review-p015":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002) proposed a dual-route past-tense model with a lexicon for irregulars and a grammar for regulars.** The input is a **word stem** (walk, hold) and a **grammatical feature** (past tense).","children":["The **lexicon** uses lookup and association, giving held.","The **grammar** combines stem and suffix, giving walk plus -ed.","A **blocking** link lets a retrieved irregular stop the regular form."]},{"text":"The two routes match two memory systems.","children":["The lexicon is **declarative memory** (words and facts) in temporo-parietal cortex.","The grammar is the **procedural system** (rules and skills) in frontal cortex and basal ganglia."]},{"text":"It resembles the reading dual-route model.","children":["Both split **word-specific knowledge** from a **productive operation**."]},{"text":"It also differs.","children":["Reading turns spelling into sound.","This model changes a stem by a grammatical feature."]}],"m3-review-p016":[{"text":"**The past-tense dual-route model looks like the reading model, but it came from different motives.**","children":[]},{"text":"The reading model came from patient data.","children":["It was built on **neuropsychological double dissociations** (patients whose impairments pattern in opposite ways)."]},{"text":"The past-tense model came from linguistics.","children":["**Pinker and colleagues** were motivated by other concerns.","**Generative linguistics** of the time was mostly about categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars.","These rules often have **exceptions**.","So the model keeps exceptions apart from the rules."]}],"m3-review-p017":[{"text":"**The dual-process model gives exceptions their own process, so the grammar can stay elegant.** Linguists often left exceptions out of the grammar rather than add machinery for them.","children":[]},{"text":"The second process has every property the rule route lacks.","children":["It is **word-specific** (lexical).","It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence.","It is **sensitive to phonological similarity**."]},{"text":"Similar irregulars form families.","children":["Keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt share a pattern.","The grammar stays symbolic and categorical."]}],"m3-review-p018":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987) proposed that the past tense is all associative learning.** One trainable network learns past tenses across all verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"The network has three parts.","children":["It encodes a verb root's sound as **Wickelfeatures**.","A **pattern associator** with modifiable connections maps it onto a past-tense pattern.","The output pattern is decoded into a past-tense form."]},{"text":"There is no separate -ed rule.","children":["**Regular forms** are a pattern learned from many examples.","**Irregular forms** are learned in the same system."]}],"m3-review-p019":[{"text":"**Children learn past tenses in a U shape, getting worse before they get better.** Rumelhart and McClelland's model targeted this **U-shaped learning**, because early learning models focused on development.","children":[]},{"text":"The three stages come in order.","children":["Children learn some frequent irregular verbs quite early, such as run-ran.","Then, spontaneously, children produce **regularizations** (regular endings on irregular verbs) such as runned.","Eventually, children produce correct forms again."]},{"text":"Performance goes down and then back up.","children":["That dip and recovery gives the U shape."]}],"m3-review-p020":[{"text":"**The words and rules approach explains U-shaped learning as a series of distinct stages and processes.** This is why U-shaped learning matters to it.","children":[]},{"text":"Each stage has its own process.","children":["Early correct forms are just **imitations**. They do not use adult mechanisms.","When the **rule** is first learned, it is applied to all cases.","The rule is the best hypothesis for all items generally, so runned appears.","Over time, **exceptions** are learned associatively, one by one.","The **blocking process** develops, so a stored irregular blocks the rule."]}],"m3-review-p021":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland's single network produced U-shaped learning because its training input changed over time.** The **training set** is based on data about input to children.","children":[]},{"text":"Early training is mostly irregular.","children":["The highest-frequency verbs tend to be irregular, such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","The model learns these irregular forms first."]},{"text":"Later training adds regulars.","children":["As vocabulary expands, more **regular forms** enter.","The model reorganizes to capture those regularities.","This **interferes** with some early-learned forms.","With further training, the irregular forms recover."]}],"m3-review-p022":[{"text":"**Because the model learned only a few verbs, real English verbs could serve as a \"wug\" test of generalization.** A **wug test** asks for the past tense of a word the learner has not learned.","children":[]},{"text":"The test used **unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs**.","children":["Many responses were correct, such as guarded, mapped and typed.","Some were odd, such as shipped for shape."]},{"text":"Feature accuracy was high.","children":["The model generated **92 percent** of **Wickelfeatures** correctly for regular verbs.","It generated 84 percent for irregular verbs and 91 percent overall.","Correct features do not guarantee a fully correct word."]}],"m3-review-p023":[{"text":"**The model sometimes gave bizarre past tenses that people would be unlikely to produce.** Just four responses added or changed consonants.","children":["Maded for mate.","Squawked for squat.","Membled for mail.","Toureder for tour."]},{"text":"The authors responded in three ways.","children":["They said humans are unlikely to make these errors, especially the last two.","They noted that most of these responses were **near threshold**.","They suggested an **auto-associative network** among output units to clean up the output.","They had not yet implemented it."]}],"m3-review-p024":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999) proposed that phonology supports regular generalization and semantics supports irregular forms.** Their model links **speech input**, **semantics** (word meaning) and **speech output**.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has two premises.","children":["**Rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations**.","**Irregular forms** require greater support from **semantics**."]},{"text":"So the regular and irregular difference comes from shared systems.","children":["No separate rule route is needed."]}],"m3-review-p025":[{"text":"**The Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999) model aims to explain why some patients fail on irregulars and others fail on nonwords.** This target is a **dissociation** in neuropsychological data.","children":[]},{"text":"Each item type leans on a different support.","children":["**Nonwords** have no meaning, so they depend on **phonology**.","**Irregulars** break the regular pattern, so they depend more on **semantics**."]},{"text":"Damage predicts opposite patterns.","children":["Phonological damage should hurt nonwords most.","Semantic damage should hurt irregulars most."]}],"m3-review-p026":[{"text":"**Patients with Broca's aphasia are worst at putting nonwords into the past tense.**","children":[]},{"text":"The syndrome has three features.","children":["Injury is to **left anterior** brain regions.","**Speech production** is poor.","**Regular morphology** is impaired, especially for **nonwords**."]},{"text":"Typical items are wug to wugged and blick to blicked.","children":["Nonwords were about 69 percent correct.","Regular verbs were about 82 percent correct.","Irregular verbs were about 85 percent correct."]},{"text":"A nonword has no stored past tense.","children":["So this pattern points to a **phonological** problem."]}],"m3-review-p027":[{"text":"**Wernicke's, Alzheimer's and anomic patients are worst at irregular past tenses.**","children":[]},{"text":"The anomic patients have injury to inferior or middle temporal brain regions.","children":[]},{"text":"Their scores reverse the Broca's pattern.","children":["Irregular verbs were about 60 percent correct.","Regular verbs were about 89 percent correct.","Nonwords were about 84 percent correct."]},{"text":"Irregulars need **semantic** support.","children":["So this pattern fits damage to semantics."]}],"m3-review-p028":[{"text":"**The classical aphasia dichotomy treats Wernicke's aphasia as fluent nonsense and Broca's aphasia as halting grammar-poor speech.**","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke's aphasia** gives \"word salad\" or \"Jabberwocky.\"","children":["It often includes **neologisms** (made-up words).","Syntax is roughly intact.","One patient said \"My wires don't hire right\" about his poor vision.","Another called a fork \"a need for a schedule.\""]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** gives **telegraphic** speech.","children":["Patients have trouble with **word order** and **grammatical particles**.","Important **content words** are relatively spared.","One patient's answer was fragments like \"Two doctors ... and ah ... teeth.\""]}],"m3-review-p029":[{"text":"**Broca's own patients would not necessarily count as Broca's aphasics today.** The dichotomy treats **Wernicke's aphasia** as a lexicon activation problem, perhaps inhibition. It treats **Broca's aphasia** as a **grammar** problem.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca described his cases differently.","children":["His patient **Leborgne** (\"Tan\") could only produce one jargon syllable.","He seemed otherwise in possession of his senses.","He seemed to understand what was said."]},{"text":"These patients were arguably unable to produce speech at all.","children":["The cause was apparently **motor control**.","Today they would be classified as having **apraxia of speech** (trouble planning speech movements)."]}],"m3-review-p030":[{"text":"**The aphasia dichotomy is appealing because it matches the split between grammar and lexicon in formal linguistics.** Late 20th century formal linguistics drew this sharp distinction.","children":["**Wernicke's aphasia** is a problem with activation in the **lexicon**.","**Broca's aphasia** is a problem with **grammar**."]},{"text":"It starts with **Chomsky's** formulation of the **computational problem of language learning**.","children":["A new sentence is recognized because the internalized grammar generates it.","It does not have to match a familiar sentence."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** argued for the **independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["\"Colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" is grammatical.","\"Furiously sleep ideas green colorless\" is not.","Neither had occurred in an English speaker's experience."]}],"m3-review-p031":[{"text":"**The speech production model splits an arbitrary word-meaning level from a structural grammar level.** These are the **functional** and **positional** levels.","children":[]},{"text":"The levels run in order.","children":["The **message level** arouses concepts and event structure.","The **functional level** arouses word meanings and semantic roles.","The **positional level** puts word forms in grammatical order.","**Phonological encoding** and **articulation** follow."]},{"text":"The levels differ in kind.","children":["**Lemmas** (word representations chosen for meaning) relate arbitrarily to their referents.","**Syntactic trees** and **affixes** come from abstract syntactic processes."]},{"text":"HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE seem non-arbitrary.","children":["But errors like \"easy enoughly\" place **derivational morphology** at the positional level."]}],"m3-review-p032":[{"text":"**Most languages describe nearby space egocentrically, but Tzeltal uses absolute directions.** **Egocentric** terms use the speaker's body, such as left, right, in front and behind.","children":[]},{"text":"**Tzeltal** is a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas.","children":["It uses **absolute directions** (directions that stay fixed when a person turns).","A few other languages do too, mostly unrelated and broadly distributed.","Examples are found in Bali and Queensland."]},{"text":"To an English speaker, \"raise your east hand\" feels unnatural.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p033":[{"text":"**Tzeltal's uphill and downhill are used as fixed cardinal directions, which makes the system absolute.** The system is historically **landmark-based**.","children":[]},{"text":"Objects are placed by slope terms.","children":["Things are **uphill** or **downhill** from one another.","They are not left or right of the speaker or listener."]},{"text":"The terms act as **cardinal directions**.","children":["They come from the landscape around Tenejapa.","They do not simply point to the nearest hill.","**Levinson and colleagues (2002)** described the system."]}],"m3-review-p034":[{"text":"**In a nonverbal memory task, Dutch speakers used relative space and Tzeltal speakers mostly used absolute space.** **Majid and colleagues (2004)** used the **chips task**.","children":[]},{"text":"The task has three steps.","children":["A participant sees a card with a large and a small dot at Table 1.","After a 30-second delay, the participant is **rotated 180 degrees** to Table 2.","The participant picks the matching card from four."]},{"text":"The rotation splits the two answers.","children":["A **relative** choice keeps the layout fixed to the body.","An **absolute** choice keeps the layout fixed to the world, such as north."]},{"text":"The results matched each language.","children":["**Dutch** responses were overwhelmingly relative.","**Tzeltal** responses were mostly absolute."]}],"m3-review-p035":[{"text":"**Dutch and Tzeltal speakers also used their own spatial frames when combining relations to make an inference.** **Majid and colleagues (2004)** used a transitive inference task.","children":[]},{"text":"The steps run across two tables.","children":["Table 1 shows a cube and a cone.","After a 180-degree turn, Table 2 shows the cube and a cylinder.","Back at Table 1, the participant places the cylinder next to the cone."]},{"text":"The two frames predict different placements.","children":["**Relative** coding puts the cylinder to the right of the cone.","**Absolute** coding puts the cylinder to the south of the cone."]},{"text":"**Dutch** participants gave relative responses.","children":["**Tzeltal** participants gave absolute responses."]}],"m3-review-p036":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman argued that the testing setting, not only language, could explain the Tzeltal results.** They tried **inducing landmark-based similarity in English speakers**.","children":[]},{"text":"The settings differed.","children":["**Tzeltal speakers** were tested outside, with visible, familiar **landmarks**.","Typical psychology lab studies happen in **windowless rooms**."]},{"text":"Landmarks can encourage absolute coding.","children":["A windowless room makes relative coding more convenient."]}],"m3-review-p037":[{"text":"**English speakers used relative coding less when they could see landmarks.** **Li and Gleitman** varied the view during testing.","children":[]},{"text":"Three conditions gave different preferences.","children":["With the **blinds down**, English speakers overwhelmingly chose **relative** position, like the Dutch.","With the **blinds up** and a street view, the preference was **less pronounced**.","**Outdoors**, the preference was also less pronounced."]},{"text":"Only the setting changed.","children":["The language group stayed the same.","A weaker preference is not a full switch to absolute coding."]}],"m3-review-p038":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues argued that Li and Gleitman's task made its purpose too easy to guess.**","children":[]},{"text":"They raised a task objection.","children":["Li and Gleitman made the rotation too **transparent**.","They **reduced memory load**.","They shortened the **time and distance** between stimulus and test."]},{"text":"They raised a participant objection.","children":["The participants were **Ivy League students**.","Such students excel at figuring out what the experimenter wants and making it happen."]}],"m3-review-p039":[{"text":"**Researchers asked whether signers show categorical perception for hand configuration.** **Categorical perception** means a gradual change is perceived as a sudden switch between categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Two continua were made.","children":["The **phonemic** continuum runs from the sign PLEASE to the sign SORRY.","The **allophonic** continuum runs from open-N to closed-N within the sign SAY-NO-TO."]},{"text":"The predictions are specific.","children":["Categorical perception predicts a sharp **identification** boundary.","It predicts the best **discrimination** at that boundary.","It should appear only for the phonemic continuum."]}],"m3-review-p040":[{"text":"**Deaf signers perceive the phonemic hand contrast categorically, but not the allophonic one.** The curves have the familiar categorical shape.","children":[]},{"text":"The **phonemic** condition is sharp.","children":["Identification has a fairly sharp **boundary** between stimuli 4 and 6.","The boundary matches the **peak** in the **discrimination function**."]},{"text":"The **allophonic** condition is shallow.","children":["The identification function is much shallower, with many more ambiguous stimuli.","The discrimination function has **no peak**."]}],"m3-review-p041":[{"text":"**Sign language comprehension uses the same core language areas as speech, plus visual and spatial areas.** **Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people perceiving audio-visual speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign is similar to speech.","children":["It is **left lateralized**.","It uses **Broca's area** (inferior frontal) and **Wernicke's area** (posterior superior temporal)."]},{"text":"Sign is different from speech.","children":["It uses more **visual areas** and fewer **auditory areas**.","It uses **superior parietal** cortex, a classic spatial area."]}],"m3-review-p042":[{"text":"**Sign language is a true language, and learning it also reshapes the brain.**","children":[]},{"text":"Four findings support this.","children":["Sign has **phonological structure**, and native signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features.","Learning sign causes **brain reorganization**, especially **lateralization of motion processing**.","Only **deaf signers** show enhanced visual attention in the **periphery**, though the periphery matters to all signers.","Signed sentences activate **posterior STG** (Wernicke's area) more than organized, sequenced non-language hand movements do."]}],"m3-review-p043":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989) see the maturational state hypothesis as the better fit to their data.** Both versions of the **critical period hypothesis** start from a superior early capacity for languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The versions differ on what causes decline.","children":["The **exercise hypothesis** says unused capacity declines with maturation.","It says exercised capacity stays intact for life.","The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity declines with maturation regardless."]},{"text":"Their data favor maturation.","children":["Their late learners had exercised the capacity on a first language.","Late starters still did worse on the second language."]},{"text":"This changes the reading of **L1 critical period** data.","children":["Late L1 learners never exercised the capacity.","So both versions predict their poor outcome.","Early L1 experience still matters."]}],"m3-review-p044":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002) found that a first language learned late is learned poorly, while an early language helps later learning.**","children":[]},{"text":"The groups differed in early experience.","children":["Many **deaf children** get no sign input but have an otherwise typical upbringing.","Children aged 9 to 15 struggle with **ASL** (American Sign Language) as a first language.","Children who went deaf after learning a **spoken language** do much better.","The differences **persist into adulthood**."]},{"text":"Adult scores matched this.","children":["On ASL recall, no early language gave about 36 percent. Early spoken language gave about 78 percent.","On English grammar, no early language gave about 52 percent, near chance. Early signed or spoken language gave about 80 percent."]}],"m3-review-p045":[{"text":"**The semantic blocking effect is not likely due to visual similarity between same-category objects.** The **semantic blocking effect** is slower naming when same-category objects are named together.","children":[]},{"text":"The worry is a **confound** (an alternative cause).","children":["Same-category objects might look alike.","So the effect might arise in **visual object recognition**."]},{"text":"Two responses answer it.","children":["The stimuli were **normed**, so same-category objects were not more visually similar on average.","A **control experiment** blocked by visual similarity and found different results.","The visual effect appeared in the **first block of trials**.","The semantic effect appears only after the first cycle."]}],"m3-review-p046":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues showed four objects at once but had people name them one by one.**","children":[]},{"text":"The design answers an earlier null result.","children":["**Hermens and colleagues** showed multiple objects simultaneously and found no blocking effect.","Maybe blocking depends on seeing objects in serial."]},{"text":"The new design separates viewing from naming.","children":["Participants viewed **four items simultaneously**.","They produced the names **in serial**.","Naming still requires one selection after another."]}],"m3-review-p047":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues recorded eye movements to time each stage of naming.**","children":[]},{"text":"Two measures come from each object.","children":["**Gaze duration** is how long the eyes stay on an object.","**Eye-speech lag** is the time from leaving an object to starting its name."]},{"text":"The eyes run ahead of speech.","children":["A speaker may look at the next object before saying the current name.","So looking and speaking are separate events."]}],"m3-review-p048":[{"text":"**The blocking effect appears only in gaze duration, so it arises at lemma selection.** **Homogeneous** blocks (same category) are compared with **heterogeneous** blocks (mixed).","children":[]},{"text":"Each measure maps to a stage.","children":["**Speech duration** decreases over the experiment with no blocking effect. It reflects the **gesture level**.","**Gaze duration** shows a blocking effect after the **first cycle**. It reflects **lemma selection**.","**Lag** shows no effect. It reflects **phonological encoding**."]},{"text":"The lag result is not a reversal.","children":["Numerically it goes the opposite way.","This difference is **not significant**."]}],"m3-review-p049":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003) asked whether phoneme identification is thresholded or cascaded during word recognition.**","children":[]},{"text":"The two options differ.","children":["**Thresholded** processing (the lumber mill) passes on only a finished category.","**Cascaded** processing (the food web) passes graded information along."]},{"text":"They used eye tracking with a **visual world** display.","children":["Listeners heard a **/b/ to /p/ continuum**.","It varied **voice onset time** (VOT, the delay between consonant release and voicing).","Displays held a target (pear), a competitor (bear) and two fillers."]}],"m3-review-p050":[{"text":"**Mouse choices between bear and pear formed a typical step-like identification function.** **McMurray and colleagues (2003)** had participants click the item most consistent with the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The choices were categorical.","children":["Short VOTs gave /b/ responses (bear).","Long VOTs gave /p/ responses (pear).","The **category boundary** was about 17.5 ms."]},{"text":"A step-like final choice can hide graded processing.","children":["Eye movements measure the processing before the choice."]}],"m3-review-p051":[{"text":"**Eye movements show that ambiguity is gradient, with more competitor looks near the boundary.**","children":[]},{"text":"The gradient holds within each category.","children":["After a **bear** choice, VOTs nearer the boundary drew more looks to the pear.","After a **pear** choice, VOTs nearer the boundary drew more looks to the bear.","VOT mainly changes how **long** the competitor stays active."]},{"text":"So fine phonetic detail reaches **lexical activation**.","children":["That supports **cascaded** processing."]}],"m3-review-p052":[{"text":"**Competitor looks rise steadily as VOT approaches the category boundary from either side.**","children":[]},{"text":"The effect is clearer step by step.","children":["For **bear responses**, looks to the pear rise from 0 ms toward the boundary.","For **pear responses**, looks to the bear rise from 40 ms toward the boundary."]},{"text":"It holds across time.","children":["The **early window** is 300 to 1100 ms.","The **late window** is 1100 to 1900 ms.","Both windows show the gradient."]},{"text":"So the result favors **cascaded** processing over thresholded processing.","children":[]}]}};
